pages.text:
An engine first parses source text into an abstract syntax tree, reporting syntax errors before anything executes. It then compiles the tree into bytecode, a compact intermediate form, and begins executing. Modern engines add a just-in-time compiler: code that runs often is recompiled into optimised machine code using assumptions about the types it sees. If those assumptions later fail, the optimised version is discarded and execution falls back to a slower path, which is why writing functions that always receive the same shapes of data can be dramatically faster.

Parsing is eager at the top level and lazy for functions: a function body may be parsed only when it is first called. This keeps startup fast for large programs. The practical consequence is that a syntax error inside a function might only surface when that function runs, while a top-level syntax error stops the whole script from loading.

### Code

One thread, one call stack, and two queues. The output order is not the order you wrote:

```js
console.log('1 sync');
setTimeout(() => console.log('4 macrotask'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('3 microtask'));
queueMicrotask(() => console.log('3b microtask'));
console.log('2 sync');

// 1 sync
// 2 sync
// 3 microtask
// 3b microtask
// 4 macrotask
```

Every microtask drains before the next macrotask, so a chain of promises can starve timers:

```js
const block = (ms) => {
  const end = Date.now() + ms;
  while (Date.now() < end) { /* spin */ }
};

setTimeout(() => console.log('timer ran after'), 0);
block(120);                       // the whole event loop is frozen for 120ms
console.log('sync work done');
// sync work done
// timer ran after
```

Node and the browser both expose a way to see the effect from the inside: `performance.now()` is high-resolution, and blocking for 120ms shows up as a 120ms gap between two timestamps with no other work in between.

### The runtime: stack, heap and queues

Execution happens on a call stack of frames, one per active function call, with primitive values and references stored in frames. Objects, arrays, functions and other reference values live in a garbage-collected heap, and a variable that holds an object actually holds a reference to it. Deep recursion can exhaust the stack and throw a range error, which is why deep loops should usually be iterative.

Alongside the stack, the runtime keeps queues. Timers, network responses and user input enqueue tasks; promise reactions enqueue microtasks. The event loop repeatedly runs the current task to completion, then drains all microtasks, then picks the next task. Understanding this single-threaded model explains why a long synchronous loop freezes a page, and why promise callbacks can run before a timer that was scheduled earlier.

### Engines and compatibility

The major engines are V8 in Chrome, Edge and Node, SpiderMonkey in Firefox, and JavaScriptCore in Safari. They agree on the standard, so the language behaves the same, but they differ in performance characteristics, debugging tools and how quickly new features arrive. Safari in particular historically lags on new syntax, so shipping code should target the slowest engine you support or be compiled down by a tool such as Babel or a bundler.

Feature detection is more robust than version detection. Instead of guessing the browser, test the capability directly: check whether a function exists before calling it, or use optional chaining and fall back. This keeps code working as engines converge and avoids brittle user-agent parsing, which is discouraged because user-agent strings are easily spoofed and often misleading.

Source text is a sequence of Unicode characters. JavaScript is case sensitive, so total and Total are different names. Whitespace and line breaks separate tokens and are otherwise ignored, which is why pretty formatting has no runtime effect. Identifiers begin with a letter, an underscore or a dollar sign and may contain digits after the first character; Unicode letters are allowed. Certain words such as let, class and return are reserved and cannot be used as names.

Comments come in two forms. A line comment starts with two slashes and ends at the newline, and a block comment is delimited by slash-star and star-slash and may span lines. Block comments do not nest. Comments should explain intent rather than restate code; good names and small functions remove the need for most of them. A special comment syntax at the top of a script, such as one starting with hash-bang, marks an executable script for Unix-like systems in Node.

### Code

The lexical grammar decides what is a token before the parser ever sees structure. These all print the same string, but only because of how the tokens fall:

```js
const n = 1_000_000;              // numeric separator
const b = 0b1010;                 // 10
const o = 0o17;                   // 15
const h = 0xff;                   // 255

console.log(n, b, o, h);
console.log('a'.length, '\u{1F600}'.length);  // 1 2 — code units, not characters
```

Automatic semicolon insertion does not insert a semicolon where you might hope:

```js
// WRONG — parses as return undefined;
function bad() {
  return
    { ok: true };
}

// RIGHT
function good() {
  return { ok: true };
}

console.log(bad(), good());    // undefined { ok: true }
```

Optional chaining and the nullish operator are lexical too, and they short-circuit the whole rest of the chain:

```js
const user = { profile: null };
console.log(user.profile?.name);            // undefined, not a crash
console.log(user.missing?.a.b.c);           // undefined
console.log(user.profile?.name ?? 'anon');  // 'anon'
```

### Statements, expressions and automatic semicolons

An expression produces a value, such as a number literal or a call, while a statement performs an action, such as an if or a loop. Declarations, assignments, loops and blocks are statements; function calls and operators build expressions from smaller expressions. A program is a list of statements, and a block groups statements in braces.

JavaScript inserts semicolons automatically in many places to make the language easier to write. The rule is that a semicolon is added when the next token cannot continue the current statement, and at line breaks in specific positions. This usually works but fails in a few well-known cases, most notably when a line begins with an opening parenthesis, an opening bracket, a template literal, or an operator such as plus. The safe habit is to end statements with semicolons explicitly and to avoid starting a line with those tokens.

### Strict mode

Strict mode opts into a stricter set of rules. It is enabled per script or per function with the string directive use strict at the top, and modules, classes and most modern tooling enable it automatically. In strict mode, assigning to an undeclared variable throws instead of creating a global, deleting variables is an error, duplicate parameter names are rejected, and the value of this inside a plain function call is undefined rather than the global object.

Strict mode exists to turn silent mistakes into loud errors, and almost all new code should run in it. The one behaviour worth remembering is that this becomes undefined in plain calls, which catches methods invoked without a receiver. Because modules and classes are always strict, a modern codebase is usually strict everywhere without any explicit directive.

var declares a function-scoped variable and is the original keyword. let declares a block-scoped variable that may be reassigned, and const declares a block-scoped binding that cannot be reassigned, though the value it points to may still be mutated if it is an object. Block scope means the binding exists only inside the nearest enclosing braces, such as an if or for block.

The modern default is const, switching to let only when reassignment is genuinely needed, and never using var in new code. const communicates that a name will not be rebound and prevents a class of accidental bugs. Note that const does not make objects immutable: const list equals an array still allows list.push, because only the binding is fixed, not the contents.

### Code

`var` hoists to the function and is initialised as `undefined`; `let` and `const` hoist to the block but stay uninitialised in the temporal dead zone:

```js
console.log(typeof a);   // 'function' — hoisted and callable
function a() {}

try {
  console.log(b);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.name); // ReferenceError
}
let b = 1;

console.log(typeof c);   // 'undefined' — var is initialised to undefined
var c = 2;
```

Block scope is what fixes the classic loop-closure bug:

```js
const withLet = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) withLet.push(() => i);
console.log(withLet.map((f) => f()));   // [0, 1, 2]

const withVar = [];
for (var j = 0; j < 3; j++) withVar.push(() => j);
console.log(withVar.map((f) => f()));   // [3, 3, 3]
```

`const` prevents rebinding, not mutation — the object it points at is still mutable:

```js
const list = [1, 2];
list.push(3);
console.log(list);        // [1, 2, 3]
// list = [];             // TypeError: Assignment to constant variable.
```

### Hoisting and the temporal dead zone

Before code runs, the engine scans each scope and registers its declarations. Function declarations are fully hoisted, so they can be called before their textual position. Variables declared with var are hoisted but initialised to undefined, which is why reading one before its assignment yields undefined rather than an error. Bindings declared with let and const are also hoisted but remain in a temporal dead zone until the declaration is evaluated; reading them earlier throws a reference error.

The temporal dead zone is a safety feature, not a quirk to memorise. It prevents the confusing undefined values that var produces and encourages declaring names close to where they are used. The practical rule is to declare variables at the top of the smallest block that needs them, and to rely on hoisting only for function declarations, where it is a deliberate part of the design.

### Global scope, globalThis and shadowing

Each environment has a global object that holds globally reachable values. In browsers it is window or self, in Node it is global, and the standardised name that works everywhere is globalThis. A top-level var in a script becomes a property of the global object, while a top-level let or const creates a binding in the global lexical scope that is not a property of it. Modules have their own scope and do not leak top-level names to the global object at all.

Shadowing occurs when an inner scope declares a name that already exists in an outer scope, hiding it for the duration of the inner block. Shadowing is legal and often intentional, such as a loop variable named item inside a function that also has an item parameter, but it can hide a value you meant to use. Linting rules such as no-shadow flag suspicious cases. Rely on function and block scope rather than globals to avoid collisions between parts of a large program.

The if statement runs a block when a condition is truthy, with optional else and else-if branches. Because conditions coerce, a value such as an empty string or zero takes the false branch even though it is not the boolean false. Keep conditions explicit when the falsy distinction matters, for example testing a length greater than zero rather than testing the array itself.

Guard clauses return early when preconditions fail, which flattens nesting and makes the main path obvious. A function that validates its arguments at the top and returns or throws keeps the body free of deeply indented conditionals. This style pairs well with functions that do one thing, so each branch is short and testable.

### Code

A long `if/else` chain is fine until it gets long; a lookup table is flatter and easier to extend:

```js
const TAX = { us: 0.07, de: 0.19, jp: 0.10 };
const taxFor = (region) => TAX[region] ?? 0;
console.log(taxFor('de'), taxFor('xyz'));    // 0.19 0
```

`switch` compares with strict equality and falls through without a `break`, which is occasionally useful and usually a bug:

```js
function describe(n) {
  switch (true) {
    case n < 0:  return 'negative';
    case n === 0: return 'zero';
    default:      return 'positive';
  }
}
console.log([-1, 0, 1].map(describe));    // [ 'negative', 'zero', 'positive' ]

function days(month) {
  switch (month) {
    case 4: case 6: case 9: case 11:
      return 30;
    case 2:
      return 28;
    default:
      return 31;
  }
}
console.log(days(4), days(2), days(1));   // 30 28 31
```

### switch and lookup objects

A switch compares one expression against a series of cases using strict equality and runs the matching branch until a break or the end of the block. Forgetting break causes fallthrough into the next case, which is occasionally intentional but usually a bug. A default case handles unmatched values. Switch is convenient for a handful of discrete cases and is more readable than a chain of else-if when the same value is tested repeatedly.

Switch expressions in modern JavaScript return a value with arrow syntax and no fallthrough, which removes the break hazard. For mappings from key to handler, a lookup object or a Map is often cleaner than either form, because it separates data from control flow and can be built dynamically. Choosing a lookup object also makes the set of cases inspectable and testable.

### Exceptions and control flow

Throw and try-catch-finally provide non-local control transfer. A throw unwinds the stack until a matching catch is found, running finally blocks along the way. Catching only what you can handle, and rethrowing the rest, keeps errors from being silently swallowed. The pattern try, catch, finally guarantees cleanup while still allowing the error to propagate after finally runs if it was not handled.

Do not use exceptions for ordinary control flow such as ending a loop or signalling an expected absence; they are for exceptional conditions and carry a performance and readability cost. In asynchronous code, errors do not propagate across callbacks naturally, so promises and async functions provide their own error channel that integrates with try-catch.

A for loop has initialisation, condition and update expressions evaluated in a fixed order and is the natural choice when the iteration count is known. A while loop tests the condition before each iteration and suits conditions that change unpredictably, such as reading until a stream ends. A do-while tests after the body and therefore runs at least once, which fits prompts and retry logic.

Loop variables declared with let are scoped to each iteration, so closures created inside the loop capture the value for that iteration rather than a shared variable. With var, all closures share one binding, a classic bug now largely historical. Prefer the narrowest loop that expresses the intent, and prefer array methods such as map and filter when transforming collections.

### Code

`for...of` walks values, `for...in` walks keys and includes inherited ones — which is why it needs a guard on plain objects:

```js
const arr = ['a', 'b'];
for (const [i, value] of arr.entries()) console.log(i, value);   // 0 a  1 b

const obj = { x: 1, y: 2 };
for (const key in obj) console.log(key, obj[key]);               // x 1  y 2

for (const key of Object.keys(obj)) console.log(key);            // own keys only
console.log(Object.entries(obj));                                // [ ['x',1], ['y',2] ]
```

Labelled `break` is the clean way out of nested loops:

```js
const grid = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]];
let found = null;
outer: for (const row of grid) {
  for (const cell of row) {
    if (cell === 4) { found = cell; break outer; }
  }
}
console.log(found);   // 4
```

`for` loops and array methods are not equivalent when the body is asynchronous:

```js
const ids = [1, 2, 3];

// sequential — each await resolves before the next starts
for (const id of ids) {
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 10));
}

// concurrent — all three start immediately
await Promise.all(ids.map((id) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 10))));

// forEach does NOT wait: the loop returns before any promise resolves
ids.forEach(async (id) => { await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 10)); });
```

### for-of, for-in and iterables

The for-of loop iterates the values of any iterable, including arrays, strings, maps, sets and generators, and is the modern default for visiting values. The for-in loop iterates enumerable property names including inherited ones and is intended for objects, not arrays; using it on an array yields string indices and can pick up added properties, which is almost never desired.

Iterables provide a Symbol.iterator method returning an iterator with a next method, and the loop protocol calls that method until it reports done. Understanding the protocol explains why custom objects can be made iterable and why spread and destructuring work on the same set of values. Breaking out of a for-of loop with break is permitted, which distinguishes it from forEach, whose callback cannot stop the iteration.

### break, continue and labels

The break statement exits the nearest loop or switch, and continue skips to the next iteration. Inside nested loops, break leaves only the innermost one. A label before a loop allows break and continue to target an outer loop by name, which is occasionally the clearest way to express a two-dimensional search, though extracting a function with an early return is usually tidier.

The array methods forEach, map and filter cannot be broken out of, so a loop that must stop early should use for-of or a traditional for. This is a common performance and correctness consideration: processing a million records with forEach continues to the end even after finding the match, while a for-of loop can return immediately.

A function declaration is hoisted and can be called before it appears. A function expression is a value assigned to a variable and is created when that line runs. Arrow functions are a compact expression form that inherits this from the surrounding scope instead of having its own, has no arguments object, and cannot be used as a constructor. Choose arrows for short callbacks and for methods that should capture the enclosing this, and named declarations for top-level logic where hoisting and a clear stack-trace name are helpful.

An arrow with a block body needs an explicit return, while an arrow with a concise body returns its expression automatically. Returning an object literal from a concise arrow requires wrapping the braces in parentheses so they are not parsed as a block. Arrow functions are the idiomatic way to preserve this inside callbacks such as array methods and timers.

### Code

Declarations hoist; expressions do not. Defaults are evaluated per call, not once:

```js
console.log(typeof declared);          // 'function'
function declared() {}
// console.log(typeof expr);           // ReferenceError
const expr = function () {};

const now = (stamp = new Date().toISOString()) => stamp;
console.log(now() === now());          // false — a fresh default each call
```

Rest and spread solve the arguments-object problems:

```js
const sum = (...ns) => ns.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
console.log(sum(1, 2, 3));             // 6
console.log(sum(...[4, 5]));           // 9

function tally(first, ...rest) {
  return { first, rest };
}
console.log(tally('a', 'b', 'c'));     // { first: 'a', rest: [ 'b', 'c' ] }

console.log(Math.max(...[3, 9, 2]));   // 9
const copy = [...[1, 2], 3];
console.log(copy);                     // [1, 2, 3]
```

### Parameters, defaults and rest

Parameters may have default values, evaluated when the argument is undefined, which lets callers omit trailing arguments. A rest parameter collects remaining arguments into an array and must be last. Destructuring in the parameter list pulls fields out of an object or array argument and can supply defaults and rename bindings, which makes functions that accept an options object self-documenting.

The arguments object exists in non-arrow functions and is array-like but not an array. Prefer rest parameters, which are a real array and work in arrow functions. Passing an object as a single argument lets options grow without changing the signature, a pattern used throughout the platform APIs, such as fetch with a request init object.

### Higher-order functions and composition

Functions are values, so they can be passed as arguments and returned as results. A higher-order function takes or returns a function, which enables callbacks, decorators, factories and the array methods. This is the foundation of functional style in JavaScript: small functions composed into larger behaviour rather than inheritance hierarchies.

Composition builds a new function by chaining others, applying them right to left or left to right depending on the helper. Currying turns a function of several arguments into a sequence of single-argument functions, which is useful for partial application. Memoisation caches a pure function's results by its arguments; it is only correct for pure functions and only worthwhile when calls repeat, and its cache must be bounded to avoid unbounded memory growth.

Scope is determined by where code is written, not where it is called. A nested function can read and write the bindings of every enclosing scope, and name resolution walks outward until it finds the binding or reaches the global scope. This lexical rule is what makes inner functions depend on their source position rather than on the caller.

A block introduces scope for let and const, so a variable declared inside an if or for block is not visible outside it. Function declarations create scope too. Because scope is static, a reader can determine what a name refers to by looking at the surrounding text, which is a deliberate design choice over dynamic scoping used by some older languages.

### Code

A closure is a function plus the variables it captured. Each call creates a fresh binding:

```js
function counter(start = 0) {
  let count = start;
  return {
    inc: () => ++count,
    value: () => count,
  };
}
const c1 = counter();
const c2 = counter(10);
console.log(c1.inc(), c1.inc(), c2.inc(), c1.value());   // 1 2 11 2
```

The loop-capture problem and its three standard fixes:

```js
// broken with var
const a = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) a.push(() => i);
console.log(a.map((f) => f()));        // [3, 3, 3]

// fix 1: let
const b = [];
for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) b.push(() => j);
console.log(b.map((f) => f()));        // [0, 1, 2]

// fix 2: an IIFE, which is what you used before let existed
const c = [];
for (var k = 0; k < 3; k++) c.push(((n) => () => n)(k));
console.log(c.map((f) => f()));        // [0, 1, 2]
```

### Closures

A closure is a function together with the bindings it captured from its lexical scope. When an inner function outlives the call that created it, the captured variables are kept alive, so a counter factory can return a function that increments a private variable. Closures are how private state is expressed without classes, and how partial application and event handlers remember their context.

Closures capture bindings, not snapshots of values, so a variable changed later is seen changed by the closure. This is a benefit for shared state and a hazard inside loops with var. A closure keeps its captured values reachable, which can unintentionally retain large data structures and cause a memory leak; releasing the reference when it is no longer needed lets the garbage collector reclaim it.

### Immediately invoked and module patterns

An immediately invoked function expression runs as soon as it is defined and creates a private scope, historically used to avoid polluting the global object. It is still useful for running asynchronous setup once, though top-level await and modules have reduced the need. The pattern of wrapping code in a function that exposes only a chosen interface is the module pattern.

Before ES modules, the module pattern plus closures provided encapsulation: private variables lived in the function scope and public methods were attached to a returned object. Classes and modules now cover most of those use cases, but the underlying closure mechanism is unchanged. Recognising the pattern helps when reading older codebases and libraries that predate modules.

Class syntax is a clearer way to write constructor functions and prototypes. A class has a constructor run when the object is created with new, instance methods placed on the prototype, static methods placed on the class itself, and fields that initialise per instance. Classes are strict and are not hoisted in a usable way, so they must be defined before use. They cannot be called without new, which prevents the classic mistake of invoking a constructor as a function.

Fields can be declared with an initialiser, and private fields or methods are marked with a leading hash and are truly inaccessible from outside the class. Private state replaces the closure trick of the module pattern for encapsulated data. Getters and setters are declared with the get and set keywords and run on property access, which is useful for computed or validated values but hides work behind what looks like a plain field.

### Code

Class syntax is prototype inheritance with a clearer surface, plus fields and private slots:

```js
class Account {
  owner;                       // public field, initialised per instance
  #balance = 0;                // private, truly inaccessible outside
  static count = 0;

  constructor(owner) {
    this.owner = owner;
    Account.count++;
  }

  deposit(amount) {
    if (amount <= 0) throw new RangeError('amount must be positive');
    this.#balance += amount;
    return this;
  }

  get balance() { return this.#balance; }
  toString() { return `${this.owner}: ${this.#balance}`; }
}

const a = new Account('Ana');
a.deposit(100).deposit(50);
console.log(String(a), Account.count);        // Ana: 150 1
// console.log(a.#balance);                   // SyntaxError outside the class
console.log(Object.keys(a));                  // [ 'owner' ] — #balance is not a key
```

`extends` and `super` link two prototype chains; `super` in the constructor must run before `this`:

```js
class Savings extends Account {
  constructor(owner, rate) {
    super(owner);
    this.rate = rate;
  }
  toString() { return `${super.toString()} @${this.rate}%`; }
}

const s = new Savings('Bo', 3);
s.deposit(200);
console.log(String(s));                       // Bo: 200 @3%
console.log(s instanceof Savings, s instanceof Account);   // true true
```

A mixin is a function that returns a class, which is how you compose behaviour without deep chains:

```js
const Serializable = (Base) => class extends Base {
  toJSON() { return { ...this }; }
};
class Point extends Serializable(Account) {}
console.log(JSON.stringify(new Point('Cy')));   // {"owner":"Cy"}
```

### Inheritance and super

The extends keyword links a subclass to a parent class, and super calls the parent constructor or a parent method. A derived constructor must call super before accessing this, because the instance is created by the parent. Method overriding works by defining a method with the same name, and calling super with that name invokes the parent version, which supports the template method pattern where a base class defines a skeleton and subclasses fill in steps.

Deep inheritance hierarchies are fragile because a change in the base can break many subclasses and because a subclass is coupled to every detail of its parent. Composition, where a class holds helper objects and delegates to them, is usually more flexible and is preferred by modern guidance. Reach for inheritance when there is a genuine is-a relationship and the base is designed for extension.

### Static members and class expressions

Static members belong to the class rather than to instances and are reached through the class name. Static blocks run once when the class is defined and are used for complex initialisation. Static methods often serve as factories, as in a from method that builds an instance from raw data, which is a common idiom in the standard library such as Array.from and Object.create.

A class can be an expression assigned to a variable or passed to a function, and classes are first-class values in that sense. Mixins compose behaviour by copying methods from one class onto another, offering a form of multiple inheritance without a shared base. Because classes are functions at heart, everything that works with functions such as decorators and higher-order wrappers applies to them as well.

ES modules are the standard module system. A module is a file with its own top-level scope, and it exports values with the export keyword and imports them with the import statement. Imports are live bindings, so if the exporting module updates a value the importer sees the new value, which is different from copying. Modules are strict by default, are deferred in browsers, and are evaluated once even if imported from several places.

Named exports are imported by name in braces and are statically analysed, which enables tree shaking where a bundler removes unused exports. A default export is imported without braces and is convenient for a primary value but is less tree-shakeable and harder to rename consistently. Circular imports are resolved by hoisting the bindings so that at least one side may initially see an incomplete view, which is a reason to avoid cycles.

### Code

Named exports are live bindings, so an importer sees later changes; default exports are a value:

```js
// counter.js
let count = 0;
export function increment() { count += 1; }
export { count };

// main.js
import { count, increment } from './counter.js';
console.log(count);       // 0
increment();
console.log(count);       // 1 — the binding, not a snapshot
```

The shapes you will actually write:

```js
import def from './a.js';                 // default only
import { a, b as bee } from './b.js';     // named, renamed
import def2, { c } from './c.js';         // both
import * as ns from './d.js';             // namespace object
import './setup.js';                      // side effects only

export default function () {}
export const x = 1;
export { x as y };
export * from './e.js';                   // re-export everything
```

Dynamic import returns a promise, which is how you split a bundle or load only when needed:

```js
const mod = await import('./heavy.js');
console.log(mod.default ?? mod);

// in a CommonJS file
async function loadLocale(name) {
  const { messages } = await import(`./locales/${name}.js`);
  return messages;
}
```

ES modules are strict, top-level `await` works, and `this` at the top level is `undefined` — not the global object.

### CommonJS and interoperability

Node originally used CommonJS, where a module assigns to module.exports and pulls in dependencies with require. CommonJS is synchronous, evaluated on demand, and copies values rather than sharing live bindings. Node now supports ES modules natively, choosing the system per file based on the file extension and the type field of package.json, and both systems can coexist in one project.

When ES modules import from CommonJS, the default import receives module.exports as a whole, and named imports work only if the bundler or runtime can statically detect them. The reverse direction requires dynamic import, which returns a promise. These rules are the main source of confusion when a dependency ships CommonJS; identifying the module format of a package explains most interop errors.

### Dynamic import and code splitting

The import function, called with parentheses, loads a module asynchronously at runtime and returns a promise for its namespace. This enables conditional loading, lazy route components and code splitting so a large application downloads only the code a user needs. Because it is a function call, it can appear anywhere and can take a computed specifier, unlike the static import declaration.

Bundlers detect dynamic imports and produce separate chunks, which is why the pattern is central to performance work. Loaded modules are cached, so repeated calls resolve immediately after the first. Use dynamic import for features that are large and used by few users, and static imports for the code on the critical path, keeping the initial bundle small.

An iterable is any object with a Symbol.iterator method returning an iterator, which is an object with a next method that returns a value and a done flag. Arrays, strings, maps, sets and typed arrays are iterable, which is why for-of, spread and destructuring all work on them. Making a custom object iterable is a matter of implementing that method, usually returning an object whose next closure tracks the position.

The loop protocol is lazy: values are produced on demand, so an iterator over an infinite sequence is usable as long as the consumer stops. Returning an object from the iterator's return method lets cleanup run when a for-of loop breaks early. This protocol is the mechanism behind for-of and is worth implementing deliberately for tree and graph structures that benefit from on-demand traversal.

### Code

Any object with a `[Symbol.iterator]` method can be used in `for...of`, spread and destructuring:

```js
class Range {
  constructor(start, end, step = 1) { Object.assign(this, { start, end, step }); }
  *[Symbol.iterator]() {
    for (let i = this.start; i <= this.end; i += this.step) yield i;
  }
}

const r = new Range(1, 5, 2);
console.log([...r]);              // [1, 3, 5]
console.log(Math.max(...r));      // 5
const [first, second] = r;
console.log(first, second);       // 1 3
```

A generator function returns an iterator whose `next()` drives the body, and `yield` is a two-way channel:

```js
function* chat() {
  const name = yield 'what is your name?';
  yield `hello ${name}`;
  return 'done';
}
const it = chat();
console.log(it.next());                 // { value: 'what is your name?', done: false }
console.log(it.next('Ana'));            // { value: 'hello Ana', done: false }
console.log(it.next());                 // { value: 'done', done: true }
```

Because generators are lazy, an infinite one is safe as long as you stop taking:

```js
function* naturals() {
  let n = 0;
  while (true) yield n++;
}
function* take(iterable, n) {
  let i = 0;
  for (const value of iterable) {
    if (i++ >= n) return;
    yield value;
  }
}
console.log([...take(naturals(), 5)]);   // [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
console.log([...take(naturals(), 3)]);   // [0, 1, 2] — a fresh generator each time
```

### Generator functions

A generator function is declared with an asterisk and uses yield to pause and resume. Calling it returns a generator object that is both an iterator and an iterable, so it can be consumed with for-of, spread or manual next calls. Each yield suspends the function, preserving its local state, which makes generators an elegant way to express sequences, lazy evaluation and state machines.

A generator can receive values through next, making it a two-way channel, and yield star delegates to another iterable, flattening nested generators. Because generators are lazy they can represent pipelines without allocating intermediate arrays, though plain array methods are usually clearer for small data. Generators also form the basis of async iterators, where each value is awaited.

### Async iteration

An async generator is declared with an asterisk and awaits its yields, producing an async iterable that is consumed with for await-of. This is the standard way to process a stream of values that arrive over time, such as paginated API results, streamed file chunks or server-sent events, without buffering everything in memory. Each iteration awaits the next value before the loop body runs.

Async iteration composes with cancellation and backpressure: the consumer controls the pace because the producer yields only when asked. When reading from a stream, break out of the loop to stop early, and use try-finally or the iterator's return method to release resources. This pattern keeps memory use proportional to the current item rather than the whole dataset.

A Map stores key-value pairs where keys may be any type, not just strings, and preserves insertion order. It offers get, set, has, delete, clear and size, and iterates as entries, keys or values. A plain object is fine for simple string-keyed records, but a Map is preferable when keys are dynamic or non-string, when frequent additions and deletions occur, or when order and a reliable size matter.

A Set stores unique values and answers membership with has in roughly constant time, which makes it the tool for deduplication and for fast exclusion checks. Sets preserve insertion order and support iteration. Converting between arrays and sets with spread or Array.from is idiomatic for deduplicating a list, remembering that uniqueness follows the same-value rule, so objects are unique by reference.

### Code

`Map` takes any key and keeps insertion order; object keys are compared by identity:

```js
const m = new Map();
const key = { id: 1 };
m.set(key, 'object key');
m.set(1, 'number key');
m.set(NaN, 'NaN key');
console.log(m.get(key), m.get(NaN), m.size);   // object key NaN key 3
console.log([...m.keys()]);                    // [ { id: 1 }, 1, NaN ]
```

Building set algebra yourself is a few lines, and doing it over arrays instead is quadratic:

```js
const a = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const b = new Set([3, 4]);
const union = new Set([...a, ...b]);
const intersection = new Set([...a].filter((x) => b.has(x)));
const difference = new Set([...a].filter((x) => !b.has(x)));
console.log([...union], [...intersection], [...difference]);
// [1, 2, 3, 4] [3] [1, 2]
```

`WeakMap` holds keys weakly, which is what makes object metadata safe:

```js
const privateData = new WeakMap();

class Counter {
  constructor() { privateData.set(this, { count: 0 }); }
  inc() { return ++privateData.get(this).count; }
}

let c = new Counter();
c.inc();
console.log(Object.keys(c));        // [] — nothing exposed
c = null;                           // the entry becomes collectable
console.log(privateData.size);      // TypeError: no size — could change any time
```

### Weak collections

WeakMap and WeakSet hold their entries weakly, meaning they do not prevent their keys from being garbage collected. They accept only objects and functions as keys and are not iterable and have no size, precisely because their contents may vanish. They are the correct way to attach private data to an object, to cache derived values keyed by an object, and to track which objects have been processed without leaking memory.

A WeakMap used as a side table is the recommended replacement for the old convention of attaching a private property to an object. Because keys are weakly held, when the object is no longer referenced anywhere else the entry disappears automatically, which is why weak collections are central to caches that must not grow without bound. They cannot be enumerated, which is a limitation and also the guarantee that makes them safe.

### Choosing a collection

The choice among the collections follows the access pattern. Use an array for an ordered sequence with positional access, a Set for uniqueness and membership, a Map for keyed lookup with arbitrary keys, and a weak collection for metadata that must not keep its subject alive. Objects remain the best fit for fixed-shape records with known keys because their shape can be optimised by the engine.

Mixing structures is normal: a Map of arrays groups records by key, an array of Sets holds the unique values per group, and so on. The cost of operations differs: array insertion in the middle is linear, set and map operations are near constant, and object property access is very fast for stable shapes. Choosing correctly removes the need for most micro-optimisations and keeps code clear.

JavaScript runs on one thread per realm, so only one piece of code executes at a time. Work that cannot finish immediately, such as a timer or a network response, is handed to the host, which queues a callback. The event loop takes one task from the queue, runs it to completion, then repeats. Because a task runs to completion, a long loop blocks timers, rendering and input, which is why heavy work must be broken up or moved to a worker.

The queue is not the only structure. Rendering happens between tasks in a browser, and the host decides how often to repaint. Microtasks, which come from promise reactions and queueMicrotask, have their own queue that is drained completely after each task and before the next task or render. This ordering is the source of the difference between setTimeout with zero delay and a resolved promise: the promise callback runs first.

### Code

The order is: synchronous code, then all microtasks, then one macrotask, then all microtasks again:

```js
console.log('script start');

setTimeout(() => console.log('timeout'), 0);

Promise.resolve()
  .then(() => console.log('then 1'))
  .then(() => console.log('then 2'));

queueMicrotask(() => console.log('microtask'));

console.log('script end');

// script start
// script end
// then 1
// microtask
// then 2
// timeout
```

`await` is sugar over a microtask, so it yields the function but not the whole job:

```js
async function f() {
  console.log('f: start');
  await null;
  console.log('f: after await');
}

f();
console.log('sync continues');

// f: start
// sync continues
// f: after await
```

A blocked loop delays timers but not synchronous code, which is why long work must be chunked or moved to a worker:

```js
const spin = (ms) => { const end = Date.now() + ms; while (Date.now() < end); };

const start = Date.now();
setTimeout(() => console.log('timer fired after', Date.now() - start, 'ms'), 10);
spin(100);
console.log('blocked for', Date.now() - start, 'ms');
// blocked for 100 ms
// timer fired after 100 ms — not 10
```

### Timers and scheduling

setTimeout schedules a callback after at least the given delay, not exactly at that time, because the callback waits for the current task and any queued work to finish. setInterval repeats, and because intervals can drift and pile up when the callback is slow, a self-scheduling timeout is often more accurate. Passing zero as the delay means as soon as possible, not immediately, and still yields to the event loop.

Clearing a timer requires keeping its identifier and calling clearTimeout or clearInterval, which matters in component lifecycles and when an operation is cancelled. In browsers, requestAnimationFrame schedules work just before the next repaint and is the correct way to animate, and requestIdleCallback runs low-priority work when the main thread is free. Choosing the right scheduler keeps the interface responsive.

### Microtasks and starvation

Microtasks run after the current task and before anything else, including rendering and timers. A promise's then and catch callbacks, and the code after an await, are microtasks. A microtask that schedules more microtasks can delay rendering and timers indefinitely, a condition called microtask starvation, so recursive promises should be replaced by a task or a timer at the edge.

Because microtasks run to completion, awaiting a resolved value still defers the continuation to the next microtask checkpoint, which is why code after an await is not synchronous even for an already-settled promise. Understanding this ordering explains subtle timing bugs and is essential when mixing promises with timers, streams and rendering.

A promise represents a value that will exist later. It is pending, then fulfilled with a value or rejected with a reason, and once settled it never changes. The then method registers handlers for fulfilment and rejection and returns a new promise, which is why chains work: returning a value from a handler fulfils the next promise with it, returning another promise waits for that one, and throwing rejects the chain. The catch method is sugar for a then with only a rejection handler, and finally runs cleanup regardless of outcome.

Because then returns a promise, a chain is a sequence of transformations rather than nested callbacks, which was the central improvement over the callback style. Errors propagate down the chain until a handler catches them, so a single catch at the end of a chain can handle failures from any step. Returning a promise from inside a handler flattens it, avoiding nested await-style structures.

### Code

Chaining and error handling: a thrown error skips every `then` until a `catch`:

```js
Promise.resolve(1)
  .then((n) => n + 1)
  .then((n) => { throw new Error('boom'); })
  .then(() => console.log('skipped'))
  .catch((err) => { console.log('caught:', err.message); return 99; })
  .finally(() => console.log('always'))
  .then((v) => console.log('recovered with', v));

// caught: boom
// always
// recovered with 99
```

The four combinators fail in different ways, and that difference is the point:

```js
const ok = (v, ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms));
const bad = (ms) => new Promise((_, j) => setTimeout(() => j(new Error('nope')), ms));

console.log(await Promise.all([ok(1, 5), ok(2, 1)]));            // [1, 2] — input order
console.log(await Promise.allSettled([ok(1, 5), bad(1)]));
// [ {status:'fulfilled',value:1}, {status:'rejected',reason:Error} ]
console.log(await Promise.any([bad(5), ok('fast', 1)]));         // 'fast'
console.log(await Promise.race([ok('slow', 50), ok('quick', 1)]));   // 'quick'
```

Never construct a promise you cannot reject, and never forget that the executor runs immediately:

```js
const p = new Promise((resolve) => {
  console.log('executor runs now');
  setTimeout(() => resolve('later'), 0);
});
console.log('after construction');
console.log(await p);
// executor runs now
// after construction
// later
```

### Combinators

Promise.all waits for every promise and fulfils with an array of results, rejecting as soon as any promise rejects. Promise.allSettled waits for all and never rejects, returning a status and value or reason per item. Promise.race settles as soon as the first promise settles, whether fulfilled or rejected, and Promise.any fulfils with the first fulfilled value and rejects only if all reject. These four cover nearly every coordination need.

Choosing among them is a matter of intent. Use all when every result is required and a failure should fail the whole operation. Use allSettled when you want to process successes and failures together. Use race for timeouts, wrapping a real promise and a timer that rejects. Use any when several sources can supply the value and only one needs to succeed.

### Creating and converting promises

The Promise constructor takes an executor with resolve and reject callbacks and is used to wrap callback-based APIs. The new Promise antipattern is wrapping an operation that already returns a promise, which adds nesting without benefit. To convert a callback API, wrap it once in a small helper and reuse that helper rather than repeating the wrapper at every call site.

Prefer promise-returning APIs when available; most platforms now provide them for files, network and timers. Static helpers such as Promise.resolve and Promise.reject create settled promises, and tools like util.promisify turn a Node-style callback function into one that returns a promise. Unhandled rejections surface as warnings and can terminate a process, so every chain should end with a catch or be awaited inside a try-catch.

An async function always returns a promise; a plain return fulfils it and a throw rejects it. Inside, await pauses the function until the awaited promise settles, then resumes with the value or throws the reason. This lets asynchronous logic be written in the same shape as synchronous code, with try-catch for errors and ordinary loops and conditions.

Awaiting a non-promise value simply continues on the next microtask, so await is safe on any value. Awaiting inside a loop processes items sequentially, which is correct when the order or rate matters but slow when the operations are independent. For independent work, start all the promises first and await them together, so the calls overlap instead of running one after another.

### Code

Awaiting in sequence is a correctness tool and a performance trap; the two forms are not equivalent:

```js
const fetchOne = (id) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => r({ id }), 50));

// sequential: ~150ms total
const start1 = Date.now();
const sequential = [];
for (const id of [1, 2, 3]) sequential.push(await fetchOne(id));
console.log('sequential', Date.now() - start1);   // ~150

// parallel: ~50ms total
const start2 = Date.now();
const parallel = await Promise.all([1, 2, 3].map(fetchOne));
console.log('parallel', Date.now() - start2);     // ~50
```

Errors behave like synchronous ones, and `finally` runs either way:

```js
async function load(id) {
  try {
    if (id < 0) throw new RangeError('bad id');
    return await fetchOne(id);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('load failed:', err.message);
    return null;
  } finally {
    console.log('cleanup for', id);
  }
}
console.log(await load(-1));
```

A floating promise is a bug that never surfaces; `void` marks the ones you mean to ignore:

```js
// bad: an unhandled rejection and no way to know when it finished
function save(data) {
  db.write(data).then(() => console.log('saved'));
}

// good: return it so the caller can await or catch
function saveSafe(data) {
  return db.write(data).then(() => console.log('saved'));
}

// acceptable: deliberately fire and forget, with its own error path
void saveSafe(data).catch((err) => console.error(err));
```

### Errors, parallelism and pitfalls

A rejected await throws, so try-catch handles asynchronous failures uniformly. A forgotten await on a promise that rejects produces an unhandled rejection and a silent logical error, which is why linting rules require awaiting the result of an async call or explicitly ignoring it. Returning a promise from an async function is fine but awaiting it first gives a better stack trace.

Parallel work uses Promise.all over an array of calls, or a bounded concurrency helper when there are thousands of operations and the target cannot handle them all at once. Sequential awaits for independent calls are the most common performance mistake in asynchronous code. Do not block on a promise with a synchronous wait, and do not use an async function where a synchronous one is required, such as a callback whose return value is used directly.

### Top-level await

In a module, await may be used at the top level, so setup work such as loading configuration can finish before the module exports are used. Top-level await in one module makes every module that imports it wait, which is convenient but can serialise startup if used carelessly. It is unavailable in CommonJS scripts, where an async wrapper is required instead.

Use top-level await for genuine one-time initialisation that the module cannot function without, such as opening a database connection or fetching a remote schema. Avoid it for optional or lazy work, which belongs in a function called on demand. Because it delays dependent modules, keep the awaited work short and resilient, with a timeout or fallback so one slow dependency does not stall the entire application.

The AbortController pairs a signal with an abort method. Passing the signal to fetch, to a stream reader, or to a custom API lets the caller cancel work in progress. Aborting rejects the pending promise with an abort error, which must be distinguished from a genuine failure in the catch handler. A controller can be reused only until it is aborted; create a new one per operation.

Combining a timeout with cancellation is a common pattern: race the real promise against a timer that aborts the controller, so a slow request is cancelled rather than left running. Many APIs accept a signal directly, which is cleaner than racing. Always clean up listeners and timers when an operation finishes, so cancelled work does not leave dangling resources.

### Code

`AbortController` is how modern APIs are cancelled, and the signal is passed down rather than checked:

```js
const controller = new AbortController();
const { signal } = controller;

const work = (signal) => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  const t = setTimeout(() => resolve('done'), 500);
  signal.addEventListener('abort', () => {
    clearTimeout(t);
    reject(signal.reason);
  });
});

setTimeout(() => controller.abort(new Error('user navigated away')), 50);
try {
  await work(signal);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.message);        // user navigated away
}
```

`AbortSignal.timeout` and `AbortSignal.any` remove most of the manual wiring:

```js
const withTimeout = (ms, fn) => fn(AbortSignal.timeout(ms));
try {
  await withTimeout(20, (signal) => work(signal));
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.name);           // TimeoutError
}

const both = AbortSignal.any([AbortSignal.timeout(1000), controller.signal]);
```

Bounded parallelism is a fixed set of workers draining a shared index, and it is the only way to fan out safely:

```js
async function mapLimit(items, limit, fn) {
  const results = new Array(items.length);
  let next = 0;
  const run = async () => {
    while (next < items.length) {
      const i = next++;
      results[i] = await fn(items[i], i);
    }
  };
  await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: Math.min(limit, items.length) }, run));
  return results;
}

console.log(await mapLimit([1, 2, 3, 4], 2, async (n) => n * n));   // [1, 4, 9, 16]
```

### Web Workers and worker threads

A worker runs JavaScript on another thread. In browsers a Web Worker is created from a script URL and communicates by posting messages; in Node a worker thread does the same with a similar API. Workers share no memory by default, so data is copied or transferred. A transferred ArrayBuffer moves its ownership, which avoids a copy for large binary data and is the standard technique for high-performance pipelines.

Workers are the answer to heavy computation that would otherwise block the interface: parsing large files, image processing, cryptography, and simulation. Keep the main thread responsive by sending work out and receiving results as messages. SharedArrayBuffer with atomics allows true shared memory but requires specific security headers and careful synchronisation, and should be reserved for cases where copying is genuinely too expensive.

### Choosing a concurrency approach

Most concurrency needs are met by promises and await, with bounded parallelism to avoid overwhelming a resource. Workers are for CPU-bound work. Streams are for large or unbounded data arriving over time, where processing incrementally keeps memory constant. Cancellation should accompany any long-running or user-initiated operation.

The failure modes to design against are unhandled rejections, unbounded concurrency that exhausts connections, and blocking loops that freeze the interface. A small concurrency limiter, an abort signal on every request, and a rule that heavy computation goes in a worker together cover the majority of production incidents in asynchronous JavaScript.

The document object is the entry point to the DOM, a tree of nodes representing the page. querySelector returns the first match for a CSS selector and querySelectorAll returns a static NodeList of all matches, while the older getElementById and class-based lookups remain for speed and compatibility. Because NodeList is not an array, convert it with Array.from or spread before using array methods.

New content is built with createElement, document fragments for batching, and text nodes for safety. Setting textContent replaces a node's text safely, while innerHTML parses a string as HTML and is the classic injection vector when the string contains user input. Prefer creating elements and setting textContent, or a templating library that escapes by default, over assembling markup strings by hand.

### Code

Reading and writing the tree, and the difference between the two node collections:

```js
const list = document.querySelector('#list');
const items = document.querySelectorAll('#list li');

console.log(items.length);              // NodeList — live-ish, forEach works
console.log([...items].map((li) => li.textContent));
console.log(list.parentElement, list.children.length, list.firstElementChild);

list.dataset.role = 'menu';             // becomes data-role="menu"
list.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
list.classList.add('open');
list.classList.toggle('open');
console.log(list.className, list.dataset.role);
```

Building nodes is safer and faster than parsing HTML, and batching writes avoids repeated layout:

```js
const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
for (const name of ['Ana', 'Bo', 'Cy']) {
  const li = document.createElement('li');
  li.textContent = name;               // textContent escapes; innerHTML does not
  li.style.fontWeight = '600';
  frag.append(li);
}
document.querySelector('#list').append(frag);   // one layout, not three
```

The classic mistake is interleaving reads and writes, which forces the browser to lay out again each time:

```js
const rows = [...document.querySelectorAll('.row')];

// BAD: read, write, read, write...
for (const row of rows) {
  const h = row.offsetHeight;          // read -> layout
  row.style.height = `${h + 4}px`;     // write
}

// GOOD: read everything, then write everything
const heights = rows.map((row) => row.offsetHeight);
rows.forEach((row, i) => { row.style.height = `${heights[i] + 4}px`; });
```

### Traversal, attributes and classes

Nodes are reached through parent, child and sibling properties, with children restricted to elements and childNodes including text and comment nodes. Attributes are read and written with getAttribute and setAttribute for arbitrary names, and through reflected properties such as id and value for the well-known ones. The classList API adds, removes and toggles classes without string manipulation, and the style property sets inline styles.

Reading layout properties such as offsetWidth or getBoundingClientRect forces the browser to compute layout, and interleaving reads with writes causes repeated reflow, known as layout thrashing. Batch reads first, then writes, or use requestAnimationFrame. When many nodes change, build them off the document with a fragment and insert once, so the browser lays out the change a single time.

### Reactive updates and reconciliation

Hand-writing DOM updates works but scales poorly because the code must track which node corresponds to which piece of state. Frameworks solve this by describing the desired output as a function of state and letting a reconciler compute the minimal set of node operations, which removes the manual bookkeeping and the class of bugs where the view and the data disagree.

Even without a framework, the principle of deriving the view from state is worth following. Keep a single source of truth, render from it, and never read state back out of the DOM. When updates are frequent, coalesce them and flush on the next animation frame, and consider virtualising long lists so only visible rows exist in the document.

An event listener is registered with addEventListener, receiving the event object and running when the event fires on that element or reaches it during propagation. Events travel through capture, target and bubble phases. By default a listener runs in the bubbling phase, when the event rises from the target towards the root; passing true registers it for the capture phase, when the event descends. The stopPropagation method halts further travel and preventDefault cancels the browser's default action.

Because events bubble, a single listener on a container can handle events from many descendants, which is called event delegation. Delegation reduces the number of listeners enormously for lists and tables, and it works for elements added later. To find the relevant element, walk up from the event target to the one matching a selector, or use the closest method, then branch on the data attribute or class that identifies the action.

### Code

The third argument is an options object, and `passive` and `once` are the two you actually need:

```js
element.addEventListener('click', handler, { once: true, capture: false });
element.addEventListener('wheel', onWheel, { passive: true });   // never preventDefault
window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
const controller = new AbortController();
element.addEventListener('keydown', onKey, { signal: controller.signal });
controller.abort();                     // removes that listener
```

Delegation is what keeps a dynamic list working without re-binding:

```js
list.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  const button = event.target.closest('button[data-id]');
  if (!button || !list.contains(button)) return;
  console.log('row', button.dataset.id);
  event.stopPropagation();              // stop it reaching an outer handler
});
```

Custom events carry data in `detail` and default to not bubbling:

```js
const picked = new CustomEvent('color:picked', {
  detail: { hex: '#ff8800' },
  bubbles: true,
});
document.addEventListener('color:picked', (e) => console.log(e.detail.hex));
document.body.dispatchEvent(picked);    // #ff8800
```

`preventDefault` cancels the browser action (navigation, form submit, context menu); `stopPropagation` stops the event travelling. They are unrelated, and confusing them is why a form sometimes submits anyway.

### Common events and custom events

Mouse events include click, pointerdown and pointermove, and the pointer events unify mouse, touch and pen. Keyboard events deliver key and code values, where key is the produced character and code is the physical key, which matters for layout-independent shortcuts. Form events include input, change and submit, and scrolling fires for scroll containers. Passive listeners should be used for touch and scroll handlers that never call preventDefault, letting the browser scroll without waiting.

Custom events let a component emit its own notifications. A CustomEvent carries a detail payload and can bubble, which makes it a lightweight messaging mechanism between decoupled parts of a page. Listeners should be removed when a component is destroyed to avoid leaks and duplicate handling, using a stored reference to the same function that was registered.

### Event handling patterns

Debouncing delays handling until activity stops, which suits search-as-you-type and resize handlers. Throttling limits handling to a fixed rate, which suits scroll and pointer move. Both prevent overwhelming work during rapid input and are implemented with a timer and a stored reference, or imported from a utility library.

Prefer delegation over attaching a listener per item, use passive listeners for scrolling, and remove listeners when they are no longer needed. Avoid putting heavy synchronous work in an event handler, because it blocks input and rendering; schedule it or move it to a worker. These habits keep interaction smooth as the interface grows.

The fetch function performs an HTTP request and returns a promise for a Response. It resolves even for error status codes such as four hundred and four, so the response's ok property must be checked explicitly. The body is a stream that can be read once, as text, JSON, an array buffer or a blob, and reading it twice requires cloning the response first. Requests are configured with a method, headers, body and signal for cancellation.

Cross-origin requests are governed by CORS: the server must return appropriate headers, and certain requests trigger a preflight. Credentials are not sent by default and must be requested explicitly. Caching, redirects and referrer policy are controlled through the request init object. For progress reporting, reading the response body as a stream gives chunks and total bytes when the server provides a content length.

### Code

`fetch` only rejects on network failure, so the status check is not optional:

```js
async function getJson(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`${res.status} ${res.statusText}`);
  const type = res.headers.get('content-type') ?? '';
  if (!type.includes('application/json')) throw new Error(`unexpected type: ${type}`);
  return res.json();
}
```

POST with JSON, timeouts, and cancellation:

```js
const res = await fetch('/api/items', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Ana' }),
  signal: AbortSignal.timeout(8000),
});
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`create failed: ${res.status}`);
const created = await res.json();
```

The body is a stream and can only be read once; clone before reading twice:

```js
const r = await fetch('/api/thing');
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([r.clone().json(), r.clone().text()]);
console.log(a, b.slice(0, 20));
```

Uploads use `FormData` and never set `content-type` by hand, because the browser must add the multipart boundary:

```js
const form = new FormData();
form.append('file', fileInput.files[0]);
form.append('caption', 'holiday');
await fetch('/upload', { method: 'POST', body: form });
```

### WebSockets and server-sent events

A WebSocket is a persistent, bidirectional connection created with the WebSocket constructor, with message, open, close and error events. It suits chat, collaboration and games where both sides push data frequently, at the cost of managing reconnection, heartbeats and message framing yourself. Binary and text frames are both supported.

Server-sent events push a one-way stream from the server over a plain HTTP connection, consumed with EventSource. The browser reconnects automatically and the protocol is simple, which makes it a good fit for live feeds, notifications and progress updates where the client only listens. Choose WebSockets for two-way interaction and server-sent events for one-way streaming, and use fetch with streaming as a third option when you need request headers or POST semantics.

### Reliability and caching

Networks fail, so requests need timeouts, retries with backoff and jitter, and graceful degradation. Retry only idempotent operations unless the server supports idempotency keys, and cap the number of attempts to avoid amplifying an outage. Surface partial failures to the user rather than silently swallowing them.

HTTP caching is controlled by response headers such as cache-control and etag, and can be supplemented by a service worker for offline access. Understanding which responses may be cached and for how long removes redundant requests and improves perceived speed. A cache-busting query or a versioned asset path is used when a resource changes but its URL does not.

Cookies are small key-value pairs sent with every matching request and are the mechanism for server sessions. They support attributes such as expiry, path, domain, secure and httponly, and the httponly and secure flags should be set for authentication cookies so scripts cannot read them and they are sent only over HTTPS. Because cookies travel with requests they add overhead and should be kept small.

localStorage and sessionStorage hold string key-value pairs in the browser, with sessionStorage cleared when the tab closes. They are synchronous, limited to a few megabytes, shared across the origin for localStorage, and accessible to any script on the page, so they must not hold secrets. The structured clone step is missing, so values must be serialised to JSON, and JSON parsing must handle corruption defensively.

### Code

`localStorage` stores strings only, so everything goes through `JSON`:

```js
const save = (key, value) => localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value));
const load = (key, fallback = null) => {
  const raw = localStorage.getItem(key);
  if (raw === null) return fallback;
  try { return JSON.parse(raw); }
  catch { return fallback; }
};

save('settings', { theme: 'dark', size: 2 });
console.log(load('settings'));           // { theme: 'dark', size: 2 }
console.log(load('missing', 'default')); // 'default'
console.log(localStorage.length);
```

Storage is per origin, survives reloads, and throws when it is full or blocked:

```js
try {
  localStorage.setItem('big', 'x'.repeat(6_000_000));
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err.name);            // QuotaExceededError
}

// Safari private mode and blocked-cookie settings can make the property throw on access
try {
  window.localStorage.setItem('k', 'v');
} catch {
  // fall back to in-memory state
}
```

`storage` fires in *other* tabs, which is how you keep two windows in sync:

```js
window.addEventListener('storage', (event) => {
  if (event.key !== 'settings') return;
  console.log('another tab changed settings to', JSON.parse(event.newValue));
});
```

`sessionStorage` has the same API but a per-tab lifetime, and `document.cookie` is the only one sent to the server — but it is limited, string-only, and must be `Secure` and `HttpOnly` when it holds a session.

### IndexedDB

IndexedDB is a transactional, asynchronous object database intended for larger structured data, including files and blobs. It stores records in object stores, supports indexes for queries, and runs in a worker-friendly API. Because it is asynchronous and event-based, it is usually wrapped in a small promise helper or replaced by a modern library that offers a promise interface.

Use IndexedDB when data exceeds what local storage can hold, when you need queries and indexes, or when storing binary content offline. Design the schema with the queries in mind, version the database so upgrades run migrations, and always open a transaction for a coherent unit of work. Clearing and re-seeding is acceptable for caches, but anything user-authored needs an explicit migration path.

### The Cache API and Service Workers

A service worker is a script that runs in the background, separate from the page, and can intercept network requests for the origin. Paired with the Cache API it enables offline access, precaching of assets and custom caching strategies such as cache-first for static assets and network-first for data. Service workers require HTTPS, register asynchronously, and have their own lifecycle of install, activate and fetch events.

Because a service worker sits between the page and the network it is powerful and easy to get wrong: a stale cache can serve outdated code indefinitely. Version cache names, clean old caches on activate, and provide a way to update and reload. For many applications a simpler approach, such as storing data in IndexedDB and letting the network handle assets, is enough and avoids the complexity.

IntersectionObserver reports when an element enters or leaves the viewport or another element, which powers lazy loading of images and infinite scrolling without listening to scroll events. MutationObserver reports changes to the DOM tree, which is used to react to third-party content or to run cleanup when nodes are removed. ResizeObserver reports element size changes for responsive layout logic.

These observers are efficient because the browser batches and delivers notifications rather than making you poll. Prefer them to scroll, resize and polling loops. Remember to disconnect an observer when it is no longer needed, and to avoid doing heavy work directly in the callback, which runs on the main thread.

### Code

`IntersectionObserver` replaces scroll maths for lazy loading and reveal effects:

```js
const io = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
    entry.target.classList.add('visible');
    io.unobserve(entry.target);            // one-shot
  }
}, { rootMargin: '0px 0px -10% 0px', threshold: 0.1 });

document.querySelectorAll('.card').forEach((el) => io.observe(el));
```

`requestAnimationFrame` is the only correct place to animate, and it is throttled to the display:

```js
let x = 0;
function frame() {
  x += 2;
  box.style.transform = `translateX(${x}px)`;
  if (x < 200) requestAnimationFrame(frame);
}
requestAnimationFrame(frame);
```

Media queries, clipboard and visibility all have first-class APIs:

```js
const dark = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)');
console.log(dark.matches);
dark.addEventListener('change', (e) => console.log('now dark?', e.matches));

await navigator.clipboard.writeText('copied');
console.log(await navigator.clipboard.readText());

document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {
  console.log(document.hidden ? 'hidden' : 'visible');
});
```

`ResizeObserver` and `AbortSignal` round out the set: the first reports element size without a window resize, and the second cancels any listener or fetch in one call.

### File, clipboard and drag and drop

The File API represents user-selected files as File objects, which are Blobs with a name and metadata. Reading them uses FileReader or the file's own text and arrayBuffer methods, and large files should be read in slices or as streams to avoid loading everything into memory. A directory or many files can be read from an input element or from drag-and-drop events.

The clipboard API reads and writes text and rich data with permissions, replacing the older document.execCommand approach. Drag and drop uses dragover and drop events with a dataTransfer payload, and must prevent the default handling to accept a drop. Always validate and sanitise pasted or dropped content before using it, because it is untrusted input from outside the application.

### Other platform capabilities

The browser exposes geolocation, notifications, web crypto, canvas and WebGL for graphics, the Web Audio API, media capture and playback, the payment request API, and history manipulation for single-page applications. Each requires user permission, secure context or both, and each should be feature-detected before use because support varies by browser and version.

Feature detection means checking for the existence of the API before calling it and providing a fallback. Never assume a capability because a browser is recent, and never parse the user-agent string to decide. Progressive enhancement, where a baseline experience works everywhere and richer features are added when available, keeps an application usable across the whole range of clients.

Node.js embeds the V8 engine and adds host APIs for the file system, network, processes and timers. It provides globals such as process, console, Buffer and the URL class, and it does not provide the DOM. The process object exposes command-line arguments, environment variables, the working directory, the standard streams and the ability to exit with a code, which is how a script communicates success or failure to a shell.

Node is event-driven and non-blocking for input and output, so a single process can handle many concurrent connections, which is why it suits servers and command-line tools. Environment variables are read from process.env and are strings; they must be parsed and validated before use. Configuration should come from the environment rather than hard-coded values, so the same code runs in different deployments.

### Code

A command-line program is `process.argv` plus an exit code:

```js
#!/usr/bin/env node
const [, , ...args] = process.argv;
const flags = Object.fromEntries(
  args.filter((a) => a.startsWith('--')).map((a) => a.replace(/^--/, '').split('=')),
);
console.log(args, flags);
process.exitCode = args.length ? 0 : 1;
```

Environment variables are strings or undefined, and everything else is a conversion you must make:

```js
const port = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 3000);
const debug = process.env.DEBUG === 'true';
if (!Number.isInteger(port)) {
  console.error('PORT must be an integer');
  process.exit(1);
}
console.log({ port, debug, node: process.version, pid: process.pid });
```

In an ES module there is no `__dirname`; you derive it from the module URL:

```js
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';
import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';

const here = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));
const asset = resolve(here, '..', 'data', 'cities.json');
console.log(here, asset, join(here, '..', 'data'));
```

Graceful shutdown is a signal handler plus a flag, so a second signal can escalate:

```js
let stopping = false;
for (const sig of ['SIGINT', 'SIGTERM']) {
  process.on(sig, () => {
    if (stopping) process.exit(1);
    stopping = true;
    console.log('closing...');
    server.close(() => process.exit(0));
    setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 5000).unref();
  });
}
```

### Core modules and modern APIs

Node ships many built-in modules, including fs for files, path for path manipulation, os for system information, http for servers, events for the emitter base type, crypto for hashing and encryption, stream for streaming data, and child_process and worker_threads for running other code. These are imported by bare name, sometimes with a node prefix that makes the origin explicit.

The file system module offers callback, promise and synchronous variants. Prefer the promise-based API in asynchronous code, and use the synchronous calls only during startup where a blocking read is acceptable and simpler. Path manipulation must use the path module so separators are handled correctly across platforms, and the URL class should be used for constructing and parsing URLs rather than string concatenation.

### Events and the emitter pattern

Much of Node is built on EventEmitter, where objects emit named events that listeners subscribe to. Methods include on, once, off and emit, and the pattern underpins servers, streams and sockets. A listener added with once runs at most one time, which suits initialisation and one-off notifications. Passing an error to an emit with the name error and no listener throws, so error events must be handled.

The emitter pattern is a form of the observer pattern and is useful outside Node too, as a lightweight way to decouple producers from consumers within an application. Watch for listener leaks, where listeners are added repeatedly without being removed, and for the loss of ordering assumptions when many listeners run for one event.

The promise-based file API provides readFile and writeFile for whole-file operations, and appendFile for adding to the end. Whole-file reads are fine for small configuration and data files but load everything into memory, so large files should be processed as streams. Writing atomically is important for durability: write to a temporary file and rename it into place, so a crash mid-write does not leave a corrupt file.

Directory operations include readdir for listing, mkdir with the recursive option to create nested directories, stat for metadata, and rm with force and recursive for removal. Always handle the not-found case explicitly rather than relying on exceptions propagating, and be careful with recursive removal and user-supplied paths, which are a security risk if not validated.

### Code

The promise API covers most work, and `withFileTypes` saves a stat per entry:

```js
import { readFile, writeFile, readdir, stat, mkdir } from 'node:fs/promises';

await mkdir('out', { recursive: true });
await writeFile('out/a.txt', 'hello\n', 'utf8');
const text = await readFile('out/a.txt', 'utf8');
console.log(text.trim());

const entries = await readdir('out', { withFileTypes: true });
console.log(entries.map((e) => `${e.isDirectory() ? 'dir' : 'file'}:${e.name}`));
const info = await stat('out/a.txt');
console.log(info.size, info.mtime.toISOString());
```

The async iterable form of a stream reads a large file with constant memory:

```js
import { createReadStream, createWriteStream } from 'node:fs';
import { pipeline } from 'node:stream/promises';
import { createGzip } from 'node:zlib';

await pipeline(
  createReadStream('big.log'),
  createGzip(),
  createWriteStream('big.log.gz'),
);
```

Buffers are byte arrays with encodings, and the conversion direction matters:

```js
const buf = Buffer.from('héllo', 'utf8');
console.log(buf.length, buf.toString('hex').slice(0, 8), buf.toString('utf8'));
console.log(Buffer.from(buf.toString('base64'), 'base64').toString('utf8'));
console.log(Buffer.byteLength('héllo'));   // 6, not .length 5
console.log(buf.subarray(0, 2));           // shares memory with buf
```

Always prefer `pipeline` over `.pipe()`: it forwards errors, destroys every stream on failure, and returns a promise you can await.

### Streams and backpressure

A stream delivers data in chunks rather than all at once. Readable streams produce data, writable streams consume it, and transform streams convert between them. Piping connects a readable to a writable and handles backpressure automatically, slowing the producer when the consumer cannot keep up. This is why a large file can be processed with constant memory.

The pipeline helper chains streams and propagates errors and cleanup, which is safer than manual piping because a failure in the middle still closes every stage. Async iteration over a readable stream gives a simple for-await loop over chunks. Choose streams when the data is large or arrives over time, and simple whole-value calls when the data comfortably fits in memory.

### Buffers and binary data

A Buffer is a fixed-length view over a raw memory block, the Node equivalent of a typed array with extra conversion methods. Buffers are returned by file and network reads, and can be created from strings with an encoding, most often utf8, or from base64 for encoded content. Concatenating buffers with Buffer.concat is the standard way to assemble chunked data.

Buffers are mutable and share memory with their source, so a slice of a buffer sees later writes to the original. When handing a buffer to code that must not change it, copy first. For text processing, decode the buffer to a string, but be careful with multi-byte characters split across chunk boundaries, which requires a streaming decoder rather than per-chunk conversion.

The package manifest records the name, version, entry points, scripts and dependencies. Dependencies are needed at runtime, devDependencies only for development and building, and peerDependencies express compatible versions a plugin expects the host to provide. Scripts are the project's command interface and should include start, build, test and lint so a new contributor can run the project without reading documentation.

The lock file records the exact resolved versions of every dependency, including transitive ones, and must be committed for reproducible installs. Without it, two machines can install different trees from the same manifest. When adding a dependency, prefer a few well-maintained packages over many small ones, and check size, licence, last release and open issues, because a dependency is code you now maintain indirectly.

### Code

The fields that change how a package behaves:

```json
{
  "type": "module",
  "exports": {
    ".": { "types": "./dist/index.d.ts", "import": "./dist/index.js" }
  },
  "bin": { "mytool": "./dist/cli.js" },
  "files": ["dist"],
  "engines": { "node": ">=22" },
  "peerDependencies": { "react": "^18" },
  "sideEffects": false
}
```

Version ranges are carets, tildes and wildcards, and the difference is what you are promising:

```js
// ^1.2.3  allows 1.x.x but not 2.0.0   — the usual default
// ~1.2.3  allows 1.2.x but not 1.3.0   — tighter
// 1.2.x   allows 1.2.*
// *       anything
// 1.2.3   exactly, no range

// sorting obeying semver precedence (1.10.0 > 1.9.0)
const versions = ['1.9.0', '1.10.0', '1.2.3-alpha.1', '1.2.3'];
versions.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, undefined, { numeric: true }));
console.log(versions);
```

Scripts are the documented entry points, and `npm` lifecycle order is what makes them reliable:

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "build": "vite build",
    "test": "vitest run",
    "prepublishOnly": "npm run build && npm test"
  }
}
```

Lockfiles pin the whole tree; commit them. `npm ci` installs exactly the lockfile, while `npm install` may update it.

### Semantic versioning and ranges

A version has three parts: major, minor and patch. Semantic versioning says a major change may break compatibility, a minor change adds functionality compatibly, and a patch fixes a defect. Ranges express acceptance: a caret allows changes that do not modify the left-most non-zero digit, a tilde allows patch-level changes, and an exact version allows nothing else. Precise ranges plus a lock file give reproducible installs.

In practice, semver is a promise that maintainers sometimes break, so upgrading a major version is a deliberate task that reads the changelog and runs the test suite. Automated dependency updates help only when the tests are trustworthy. Pinning transitive dependencies through the lock file, and auditing for known vulnerabilities periodically, keeps a project healthy.

### Workspaces and publishing

A monorepo keeps several packages in one repository and links them with workspaces, so a change in one package is immediately visible to the others without publishing. This suits a library and an application that consume it, or a set of related utilities. The trade-off is build and tooling complexity, which is worth it only when packages genuinely change together.

Publishing a package involves a unique name, an entry point, and careful use of the files field or an ignore file so only the intended output ships. A provenance and licence field, a README, and a sensible set of exports make a package usable. Version and changelog management should be automated so releases are consistent and the source of each change is recorded.

A unit test exercises one unit of behaviour and asserts an expected result. The arrange, act, assert structure keeps tests readable: set up the inputs, perform the action, then check the outcome. Tests should be independent, deterministic and fast, because a slow or flaky suite stops being run. A good unit test fails for exactly one reason, so the failure message points at the defect.

Popular runners in the JavaScript ecosystem provide a describe and it structure with expect-style assertions, and many support snapshot testing where a value is compared against a stored representation. Snapshots are useful for large outputs but must be reviewed when they change, otherwise they merely freeze bugs. Prefer explicit assertions where the important properties can be named, and reserve snapshots for structured output that is tedious to assert field by field.

### Code

A test is an assertion plus a name. This is a complete runner in fifteen lines:

```js
const tests = [];
const test = (name, fn) => tests.push({ name, fn });
const assertEqual = (actual, expected, msg = '') => {
  const a = JSON.stringify(actual);
  const e = JSON.stringify(expected);
  if (a !== e) throw new Error(`${msg}\n  expected ${e}\n  actual   ${a}`);
};

test('adds', () => assertEqual(add(1, 2), 3));
test('rejects negatives', () => {
  let threw = false;
  try { add(-1, 1); } catch { threw = true; }
  assertEqual(threw, true);
});

let failed = 0;
for (const t of tests) {
  try { t.fn(); console.log('ok  ', t.name); }
  catch (err) { failed++; console.log('FAIL', t.name, err.message); }
}
console.log(failed ? `${failed} failing` : 'all passing');
```

Table-driven tests express a rule once and cover the edges explicitly:

```js
const cases = [
  [0, 'zero'],
  [1, 'positive'],
  [-1, 'negative'],
  [Number.NaN, 'unknown'],
];
for (const [input, expected] of cases) {
  assertEqual(describe(input), expected, `describe(${input})`);
}
```

Time and randomness are the two things that make tests flaky, so inject them:

```js
function makeClock(now) {
  let t = now;
  return { now: () => t, advance: (ms) => (t += ms) };
}

const clock = makeClock(0);
clock.advance(1500);
console.log(clock.now());            // 1500 — deterministic, no timers needed
```

Test behaviour through the public surface, and assert on values rather than on log output. A test that reaches into internals locks in the implementation and fails on every refactor.

### Test doubles and integration tests

When a unit depends on the network, the clock or the file system, those dependencies are replaced by doubles: stubs return canned values, spies record calls, and mocks assert interactions. Injecting dependencies makes this possible without patching globals, which keeps tests isolated. Avoid over-mocking, because a test that mocks every collaborator verifies implementation details rather than behaviour and breaks on harmless refactors.

Integration tests exercise several units together, including the real database or a faithful in-memory substitute, and catch wiring errors that unit tests miss. End-to-end tests drive the whole system through its user interface and are slow and brittle, so keep them few and focused on critical journeys. The pyramid of many fast unit tests, some integration tests and a handful of end-to-end tests balances confidence against maintenance cost.

### Coverage, types and contracts

Coverage measures which lines and branches a suite executes. It is a diagnostic rather than a goal: high coverage with weak assertions is possible, and low coverage in a critical module is a warning. Track coverage by module and treat a drop as a prompt to add tests, not as an end in itself. Mutation testing, which changes code slightly to see whether tests fail, is a stronger signal of test quality.

In addition to tests, static checks catch whole classes of defects before execution. A type checker, a linter and a formatter together enforce consistency and catch typos, unused variables, floating promises and unreachable code. Running these in continuous integration on every change makes the feedback loop short and keeps the main branch healthy.

A bundler reads the module graph, resolves imports, and produces one or a few assets a browser can load, while also transforming syntax, handling non-JavaScript assets and applying optimisations such as minification and dead code elimination. Development builds favour speed and readable output with source maps, and production builds favour size and loading performance. Code splitting produces multiple chunks loaded on demand.

The trade-off of bundling is a build step and a source map for debugging. Because a browser can now load ES modules directly, small projects may skip bundling entirely, but features such as tree shaking across dependencies, asset handling and compatibility transforms still motivate a bundler for most applications. Modern tools balance a native-speed compiler with a rich plugin ecosystem.

### Code

A bundler is configured by what it must replace, split and target:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default defineConfig({
  define: { __BUILD_ID__: JSON.stringify(process.env.GIT_SHA ?? 'dev') },
  build: {
    outDir: 'dist',
    sourcemap: true,
    target: 'es2022',
    rollupOptions: { output: { manualChunks: { vendor: ['react', 'react-dom'] } } },
  },
});
```

Dynamic import is the only reliable code-splitting signal; the bundler cannot see a static one:

```js
// one chunk, always downloaded
import { heavy } from './heavy.js';

// a separate chunk, fetched on demand
const { heavy } = await import('./heavy.js');

// route-level splitting with a name the bundler can see
const Page = (await import('./pages/Account.js')).default;
```

Linting catches the mistakes a type system cannot express:

```json
{
  "rules": {
    "no-floating-promises": "error",
    "no-unused-vars": "warn",
    "eqeqeq": ["error", "always", { "null": "ignore" }],
    "no-restricted-globals": ["error", "event", "name"]
  }
}
```

Transpilation is a target decision. If you must ship to an older runtime, pick the target and let the tool pick the transforms — hand-written polyfills for syntax features (like spread) are almost always slower than the transpiled output, and `core-js`-style runtime polyfills for APIs cannot be added for you.

### Transpilation and target selection

A transpiler rewrites modern syntax into an older equivalent, so the source can use the latest features while running in older engines. It also converts newer APIs through polyfills, which add implementations for missing built-ins. The target is chosen by the oldest environment that must be supported, and shipping to a narrower target reduces both the transform work and the output size.

Transpiling has costs: generated code is harder to read and debug, some features cannot be emulated faithfully, and the pipeline adds build time. If the target environments all support the features used, the transpiler may be unnecessary. Modern practice is to target recent evergreen browsers, reserve transpilation for the few features not yet universal, and keep an eye on the actual support matrix rather than on folklore.

### Linting, formatting and types

A linter analyses code for suspicious patterns and style violations, with rules configured per project and extensible with plugins. It catches unused variables, shadowed names, floating promises, inconsistent equality operators and many accessibility issues. A formatter decides layout automatically, removing debates about whitespace and making diffs small and reviewable. Running both automatically before a commit keeps the codebase uniform.

A type checker adds static types either through annotations or through inference, catching a large class of runtime errors at build time. JavaScript projects can adopt types gradually by checking the existing code without rewriting it, gaining most of the safety with little disruption. Types serve as executable documentation, improve editor tooling, and make refactoring safer, which is why they are common in large codebases.

A factory function returns a new object without exposing the constructor, which hides creation details and lets the returned shape vary. A module pattern wraps private state in a closure and returns only the public surface. A singleton ensures one shared instance, which in JavaScript is usually just a module-level constant rather than a special class. These patterns replace the ceremony of classical languages with closures and objects.

Structural patterns adapt and compose. The decorator pattern wraps an object to add behaviour while preserving its interface, which is a plain higher-order function in JavaScript. The adapter translates one interface into another, the facade presents a simple interface over a complex subsystem, and the proxy interposes on property access. Because objects are dynamic, most structural patterns are small functions rather than class hierarchies.

### Code

The module singleton is the only singleton you need, because the module registry already guarantees one instance:

```js
// config.js
const config = { api: '/api', retries: 3 };
export default Object.freeze(config);
```

Factory functions hide construction and make testing easy:

```js
const createUser = ({ id, name, role = 'viewer' }) => ({
  id, name, role,
  can(action) { return RIGHTS[role].includes(action); },
  toJSON() { return { id, name, role }; },
});
const RIGHTS = { viewer: ['read'], editor: ['read', 'write'], admin: ['read', 'write', 'delete'] };
console.log(createUser({ id: 1, name: 'Ana' }).can('write'));   // false
```

The strategy pattern is a function-valued property, and the observer pattern is a Set of callbacks:

```js
const pricing = {
  flat: () => 100,
  weight: (kg) => kg * 4,
  free: () => 0,
};
const price = (mode, ...args) => (pricing[mode] ?? pricing.flat)(...args);
console.log(price('weight', 9), price('nope'));    // 36 100

const listeners = new Set();
const subscribe = (fn) => { listeners.add(fn); return () => listeners.delete(fn); };
const publish = (value) => listeners.forEach((fn) => fn(value));
const off = subscribe(console.log);
publish('changed');
off();
```

A repository interface keeps storage out of the domain, and it is a plain object of functions:

```js
const memoryRepo = () => {
  const rows = new Map();
  return {
    get: (id) => rows.get(id),
    put: (row) => void rows.set(row.id, row),
    all: () => [...rows.values()],
  };
};
```

### Behavioural patterns

The observer and publish-subscribe patterns decouple a producer from its consumers by having consumers subscribe to notifications. An event emitter or a callback registry implements both. The strategy pattern swaps algorithms behind a common interface, which in JavaScript is simply passing a function. The command pattern captures an action as a value so it can be stored, queued or undone, which is the basis of undo stacks and task queues.

The state pattern models a system as a set of states with defined transitions, often implemented with a lookup object rather than a tangle of conditionals. The iterator pattern exposes traversal without revealing the underlying structure, which is now standardised through the iteration protocol. Choosing a pattern is about naming a recurring solution; applying one where a plain function suffices adds indirection without benefit.

### Architectural patterns

Layered architecture separates concerns into presentation, domain and data layers, with dependencies pointing inward so the domain does not know about the database or the interface. A repository abstracts data access behind an interface, which makes the storage swappable and the domain testable. Dependency injection supplies collaborators from outside rather than constructing them internally, which is what makes the parts replaceable in tests.

State management patterns keep a single source of truth and update it through explicit actions, so changes are traceable. Unidirectional data flow, where actions update state and state renders the view, removes the bidirectional synchronisation bugs of older model-view approaches. The specific library matters less than the discipline of one source of truth, pure updates and a clear separation between state and rendering.

A pure function returns the same output for the same input and has no side effects. Purity makes functions easy to test, safe to cache and safe to run in parallel. Side effects such as logging, network calls and mutation are unavoidable, so the goal is to push them to the edges of the system and keep the core logic pure, which is where the reasoning happens.

Immutability means never changing a value in place; instead a new value is produced. Adding spread and the array methods to that rule yields code where data flows forward and no earlier reference changes unexpectedly. Immutability also enables cheap change detection, because a reference comparison answers whether anything changed, which is the foundation of many reactive systems.

### Code

A pure function depends only on its arguments and changes nothing outside itself:

```js
const addItem = (cart, item) => [...cart, item];                 // pure
const addItemMutating = (cart, item) => { cart.push(item); return cart; };  // not

const cart = [{ id: 1 }];
const next = addItem(cart, { id: 2 });
console.log(cart.length, next.length);    // 1 2
```

Immutable updates of nested structures are a spread chain, and it is worth naming them:

```js
const state = { user: { name: 'Ana', tags: ['a'] }, count: 0 };
const updated = {
  ...state,
  user: { ...state.user, tags: [...state.user.tags, 'b'] },
  count: state.count + 1,
};
console.log(state.user.tags, updated.user.tags);   // ['a'] ['a','b']
```

Higher-order functions compose into pipelines, and the order of `filter` before `map` matters for cost:

```js
const pipe = (...fns) => (x) => fns.reduce((v, f) => f(v), x);

const activeNames = pipe(
  (rows) => rows.filter((r) => r.active),
  (rows) => rows.map((r) => r.name.trim()),
  (names) => [...new Set(names)].sort(),
);
console.log(activeNames([{ name: ' bo ', active: true }, { name: 'ana', active: false }, { name: 'bo', active: true }]));
// [ 'bo' ]
```

Folding is where functional style earns its keep, and it replaces every loop you would otherwise write:

```js
const orders = [{ sku: 'a', qty: 2 }, { sku: 'b', qty: 1 }, { sku: 'a', qty: 3 }];
const bySku = orders.reduce((acc, o) => {
  acc[o.sku] = (acc[o.sku] ?? 0) + o.qty;
  return acc;
}, Object.create(null));
console.log(bySku);      // { a: 5, b: 1 }
```

### Composition and pipelines

Composition builds behaviour from small functions: map transforms, filter selects and reduce aggregates. A pipeline applies a sequence of transformations to a value, and is expressed either as chained method calls or as a compose helper, depending on readability. The functional core keeps each step small, named and independent, which makes the whole pipeline easy to test.

Transducers and lazy sequences go further by fusing transformations so no intermediate arrays are built, which matters for very large data. Currying and partial application fix some arguments early, creating specialised functions from general ones. These techniques are powerful but add abstraction; apply them where the data volume or the reuse genuinely justifies the cost in clarity.

Memory is reclaimed automatically by tracing which objects are still reachable. An object stays alive as long as something reachable references it, so a leak in JavaScript is an accidental reference that was never released. Common causes are timers that are never cleared, event listeners that are never removed, closures that capture large data, caches that grow without bound, and detached DOM nodes still referenced from JavaScript.

Preventing leaks is a matter of lifecycle discipline: remove listeners and clear timers when a component is destroyed, bound cache sizes, and null out references to objects no longer needed. Memory profiles in the browser developer tools show allocation over time and retained objects, which turns a guess about a leak into a specific reference to fix.

### Code

Measure with a high-resolution clock and a warm-up, or you measure the JIT:

```js
const bench = (label, fn, iterations = 100_000) => {
  fn();                                     // warm up
  const start = performance.now();
  for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) fn();
  const ms = performance.now() - start;
  console.log(`${label}: ${ms.toFixed(1)}ms total, ${(ms / iterations * 1000).toFixed(0)}ns/op`);
};

bench('includes', () => [1, 2, 3].includes(2));
```

An accidental quadratic is the most common real defect, and a Set removes it:

```js
const allowed = new Set([...Array(20_000).keys()]);
const rows = [...Array(20_000).keys()].map((n) => ({ n }));

const slow = (list) => list.filter((row) => [...allowed].includes(row.n));  // O(n*m)
const fast = (list) => list.filter((row) => allowed.has(row.n));            // O(n)
console.log(fast(rows).length === slow(rows).length);
```

Allocation per item is what turns a loop hot. Reuse the accumulator:

```js
// allocates an array per row
const out = rows.map((r) => `#${r.n}`).join('|');

// one buffer, one pass
let acc = '';
for (const r of rows) acc += `|#${r.n}`;
console.log(out.length, acc.length);
```

To find out where time really goes, capture a profile rather than guessing: `node --cpu-prof app.js` writes a `.cpuprofile` that the DevTools Performance panel opens, and the widest frame at the deepest level is the leaf that owns the time. For the event loop, `monitorEventLoopDelay` reports the delay histogram — a p99 above tens of milliseconds means something synchronous is hogging the thread.

### Optimisation and measurement

Measure before optimising, because intuition about performance is usually wrong and premature optimisation adds complexity that outlives its benefit. Profiles identify the actual hot path, benchmarks quantify a change, and the size of an input often determines which algorithm matters. Optimise the algorithm and the data access pattern before micro-tuning individual statements.

Engine-level advice that does hold: keep object shapes stable so the optimiser can specialise property access, avoid deleting properties, avoid mixing types in one array, and avoid try-catch inside the hottest loop when it forces the function to be deoptimised. None of these matter until a profile points at them. Conceptually, the biggest wins usually come from doing less work: caching, batching, lazy loading and virtualising long lists.

### Perceived performance

Speed that users feel is not the same as throughput measured in a benchmark. Showing a skeleton while data loads, updating optimistically and reconciling later, prefetching what is likely next, and keeping the main thread free so interaction feels instant all improve perceived performance even when total work is unchanged. Reducing layout shifts and painting early content matters more than shaving milliseconds off a background task.

The budget approach makes this concrete: decide a limit for interaction latency, first contentful paint and bundle size, then treat exceeding it as a defect. Instruments such as the performance API and field metrics from real users tell you whether the budget is met, rather than guessing from a fast development machine.

Cross-site scripting occurs when untrusted data is treated as code, usually by inserting it into the document as HTML. Defence is to keep untrusted values as data: set textContent rather than innerHTML, use attribute APIs rather than building markup strings, and escape consistently when a template library is not doing it for you. A content security policy is a second line of defence that restricts which scripts may run.

Other injection classes follow the same principle at different targets. SQL injection is prevented by parameterised queries, never by string concatenation. Command injection is prevented by avoiding the shell and passing arguments as an array. Template injection is prevented by not evaluating user-supplied templates. In every case the fix is to separate data from the language it is embedded in, not to filter known bad characters.

### Code

Escaping must happen at the moment of insertion into the right context, never earlier:

```js
const escapeHtml = (s) => String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g, (c) => ({
  '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;', "'": '&#39;',
})[c]);

// safe: textContent does not parse HTML
element.textContent = userInput;

// safe: an attribute value, escaped
el.setAttribute('title', escapeHtml(userInput));

// NEVER: a URL built by concatenation
// el.href = `/go?url=${userInput}`;
```

Allow-list outbound URLs before using them in a link or a redirect, and parse rather than pattern-match:

```js
const SAFE_PROTOCOLS = new Set(['https:', 'mailto:']);
function safeUrl(input, base = location.origin) {
  let url;
  try { url = new URL(input, base); }
  catch { return null; }
  return SAFE_PROTOCOLS.has(url.protocol) ? url.toString() : null;
}
console.log(safeUrl('https://example.com/a'));
console.log(safeUrl('javascript:alert(1)'));     // null
console.log(safeUrl('  JaVaScRiPt:alert(1)'));   // null
```

Never build queries or commands from strings:

```js
// SQL: parameters, always
db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ? AND active = ?', [email, 1]);
// WRONG: db.query(`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '${email}'`);

// shell: an argument array, never a string
Bun.spawn(['convert', inputPath, '-resize', '200x200', outputPath]);
// WRONG: exec(`convert ${inputPath} -resize 200x200 ${outputPath}`)
```

Passwords are hashed with a slow, salted algorithm and compared in constant time; path traversal is stopped by resolving and checking the prefix, not by stripping dots; and `eval`, `new Function` and `innerHTML` with untrusted data are all code execution.

### Browser-side threats and defences

Cross-site request forgery tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending an unwanted request, and is prevented by same-site cookies, a CSRF token, or requiring a non-simple header that forces a preflight. Clickjacking embeds a page in an iframe to capture clicks and is prevented by frame-ancestors headers. Cross-origin isolation, the same-origin policy and CORS determine which scripts may read which responses.

The same-origin policy is the browser's central isolation mechanism; treat its exceptions as deliberate. Store authentication tokens so that scripts cannot read them, prefer cookies with the httponly and secure attributes for sessions, and never place secrets in client code or in local storage, because anything shipped to the browser is public regardless of obfuscation.

### Dependencies and supply chain

Most application code comes from dependencies, so the supply chain is part of the attack surface. Vulnerabilities in a transitive package can compromise an application without any change to its own code. Regular audits, automated alerts, lock files with integrity hashes and a policy of few, well-maintained dependencies reduce the exposure. Reviewing what a package does at install time, and avoiding packages that run arbitrary code in lifecycle scripts, is part of the discipline.

Defence in depth assumes any single control can fail. Validate on the server even when the client validates, treat all input as untrusted regardless of its source, apply the principle of least privilege to tokens and services, and log security-relevant events. On the client, remember that everything can be modified by the user, so no security decision may rely on client-side checks alone.

A Proxy wraps an object and intercepts operations such as get, set, has, delete and apply. This enables validation on assignment, default values for missing properties, logging, and virtual properties computed on demand. The Reflect object provides the default behaviour for each trap, so a proxy can perform an action and then delegate, which keeps semantics correct and avoids recursion.

Proxies are the basis of many reactive and observable systems, where a set trap notifies subscribers, and of APIs that need to record or restrict access. They add a level of indirection and can surprise debugging tools, so use them where an interposition is genuinely required rather than as a general-purpose convenience. Some operations cannot be intercepted, such as identity comparison, which limits what a proxy can emulate.

### Code

A `Proxy` intercepts operations, and `Reflect` is the default behaviour you fall back to:

```js
const logged = (target, label) => new Proxy(target, {
  get(obj, prop, receiver) {
    const value = Reflect.get(obj, prop, receiver);
    if (typeof value === 'function') return (...args) => {
      console.log(`${label}.${String(prop)}(${args.join(', ')})`);
      return value.apply(obj, args);
    };
    return value;
  },
  set(obj, prop, value) {
    if (typeof value !== 'number') throw new TypeError(`${String(prop)} must be a number`);
    return Reflect.set(obj, prop, value);
  },
});

const counter = logged({ n: 0, inc() { this.n += 1; return this.n; } }, 'counter');
console.log(counter.inc());
// counter.inc()
// 1
// counter.n = 'x';          // TypeError
```

Well-known symbols let your objects participate in the language:

```js
class Matrix {
  constructor(rows) { this.rows = rows; }
  get [Symbol.toStringTag]() { return 'Matrix'; }
  [Symbol.toPrimitive]() { return this.rows.flat().join(','); }
}
const m = new Matrix([[1, 2], [3, 4]]);
console.log(`${m}`, Object.prototype.toString.call(m));
// 1,2,3,4 [object Matrix]
```

Property inspection is reflection, and it is the honest way to write a generic serializer:

```js
function describe(obj) {
  return Object.entries(obj)
    .map(([k, v]) => `${k}=${v}`)
    .join(' & ');
}
console.log(describe({ a: 1, b: 'x' }));
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyNames(describe));
```

Metaprogramming has a real cost: proxies deoptimise property access, getters hide work behind syntax, and code that reads its own structure is hard to type-check. Reach for it when a library boundary genuinely needs it, not to save a few lines.

### Property descriptors and symbols

Defining properties with descriptors gives control over writability, enumerability and configurability, and accessor descriptors replace a value with getter and setter functions. This is how libraries create read-only values, hide implementation properties from enumeration, and implement lazy computation. Symbols provide non-string keys that avoid collisions, and well-known symbols hook into language behaviour such as iteration and coercion.

These tools let a value participate in language features rather than merely being operated on by them. Implementing Symbol.toPrimitive controls how an object converts to a primitive in arithmetic and concatenation; implementing Symbol.iterator makes it usable in for-of and with spread. Used sparingly, they make custom types feel native; used everywhere, they make code unpredictable.

### When not to metaprogram

Metaprogramming trades clarity for power. Code that generates code, intercepts operations or rewrites prototypes is harder to read, harder to debug and harder for tooling to analyse. Before reaching for a proxy or a dynamic construct, consider whether an explicit function, a plain object, or a small amount of duplication would communicate the intent better.

The exception is library and framework authoring, where a small amount of metaprogramming can remove a large amount of repetition for every user of the library. In application code, prefer explicitness. A useful rule is that a reader should be able to follow what happens by reading the source, without having to simulate a hidden mechanism.

The Date type stores a moment in time as milliseconds since the epoch in UTC, but most of its methods read and write in the local time zone, which creates confusion. Month numbers are zero-based while day numbers are one-based, and parsing a string is implementation-defined except for the ISO format. These inconsistencies are why date handling is a common source of defects.

Arithmetic on dates is done through timestamps, which are plain numbers. Subtracting two timestamps yields a duration in milliseconds; adding a duration produces a new date. Never use the two-argument Date constructor for arithmetic, and never compare formatted strings to determine order. Convert to timestamps, compute, then format for display, keeping the storage format separate from the presentation format.

### Code

The constructor has three unrelated meanings, and that asymmetry is where day-off bugs come from:

```js
console.log(new Date('2024-01-01').toISOString());        // date-only string is UTC
console.log(new Date(2024, 0, 1).toISOString());          // numeric form is LOCAL
console.log(new Date(Date.UTC(2024, 0, 1)).toISOString());
console.log(new Date('2024-01-01T00:00').toISOString());  // no offset -> local
```

Always check validity, because an invalid date does not throw:

```js
const d = new Date('not a date');
console.log(d.toString(), d.getTime());     // 'Invalid Date' NaN
console.log(Number.isNaN(d.getTime()));     // true — the check that matters
const parsed = new Date('2024-02-30');
console.log(parsed.toISOString());          // 2024-03-01 — overflow is normalised
```

Arithmetic and formatting, done the ways that handle time zones:

```js
const now = new Date('2024-03-31T12:00:00Z');
const plusOneDay = new Date(now.getTime() + 86_400_000);
console.log(plusOneDay.toISOString());

const fmt = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', { dateStyle: 'full', timeStyle: 'short', timeZone: 'UTC' });
console.log(fmt.format(now));
console.log(Intl.RelativeTimeFormat('en', { numeric: 'auto' }).format(-1, 'day'));   // yesterday
```

Calendar arithmetic must go through the fields, not milliseconds, because a day is not always 24 hours:

```js
const addDays = (date, days) => {
  const next = new Date(date);
  next.setDate(next.getDate() + days);     // survives a DST change
  return next;
};
console.log(addDays(new Date('2024-03-01T00:00:00Z'), 31).toISOString());
```

### Formatting, parsing and time zones

Intl.DateTimeFormat formats a date according to a locale and options for date style, time style, calendar and time zone. Intl.RelativeTimeFormat produces phrases such as three days ago, and Intl.NumberFormat and Intl.PluralRules handle numeric and grammatical localisation. These built-ins remove the need to hand-roll formats and keep output correct across regions.

Store timestamps in UTC and convert to a local zone only for display, keeping the original instant intact. Be explicit about a time zone whenever a value is shown to a user. For complex rules such as business days, daylight-saving transitions or recurring events, use a dedicated date library built on the standard time zone database rather than implementing the rules yourself, because they are subtle and change over time.

### Working with durations and schedules

A duration is a length of time and is best represented as a number of milliseconds or a structured object of components, never as a date. Converting between units is simple multiplication, but calendar arithmetic such as adding a month is not arithmetic, because months have different lengths and daylight saving changes the number of hours in a day; a date library handles these cases correctly.

Schedules and timers are separate from dates: setInterval ticks on the event loop and drifts, while a scheduled job on a server needs a durable mechanism such as a queue or a cron entry. In the browser, computing the next occurrence and using a timeout, then recomputing after it fires, is more accurate than a fixed interval. Always account for the system clock moving forward or backward and for the tab being suspended in the background.

Browser developer tools provide a debugger that can pause execution at a breakpoint, step over and into calls, inspect local and closure scopes, and evaluate expressions in the current context. A conditional breakpoint pauses only when an expression is true, which is invaluable in loops, and a logpoint prints without pausing. The call stack panel shows how execution arrived, which is usually the fastest way to understand an unexpected value.

The console methods include log, warn, error, table for tabular data, group for nesting, time for durations, count for tallies and trace for a stack. Structured logging with objects rather than concatenated strings keeps logs readable. In Node, the same inspector protocol is available, and a debugger can be attached to a running process or a test run.

### Code

Console methods that format instead of dump:

```js
const rows = [{ id: 1, name: 'Ana' }, { id: 2, name: 'Bo' }];
console.table(rows);
console.time('loop');
for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {}
console.timeEnd('loop');
console.group('request');
console.log('id', 7);
console.groupEnd();
console.assert(1 === 2, 'this prints only when false');
console.dir({ nested: { deep: true } }, { depth: null, colors: true });
```

Stack inspection and error context:

```js
function inner() { console.trace('from here'); }
function outer() { inner(); }
outer();

try {
  throw new Error('root cause');
} catch (err) {
  const wrapped = new Error('while saving', { cause: err });
  console.error(wrapped.message, '->', wrapped.cause.message);
  console.error(wrapped.stack.split('\n').slice(0, 3).join('\n'));
}
```

A breakpoint beats a log line for control flow, and it needs no redeploy:

```js
function total(items) {
  debugger;                       // pauses when devtools is open
  return items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.qty * i.price, 0);
}
```

In Node the same thing is available over the inspector: `node --inspect-brk app.js` then attach a client. Add `--enable-source-maps` so the stack points at your TypeScript instead of the bundle, and group production errors by fingerprint — message plus top frame — so one bug does not create a thousand report items.

### Network, performance and memory panels

The network panel shows every request with its headers, payload, timing and response, which is the first stop for a slow page or a failing API call. The waterfall view reveals serialisation that could be parallel, and the size column reveals assets that should be compressed or split. Filtering by type and by status quickly isolates the request at fault.

The performance panel records a trace of JavaScript, layout, paint and idle time, exposing long tasks that block interaction. The memory panel takes heap snapshots and allocation timelines to find retained objects and confirm a leak. The application panel inspects storage, caches, cookies and service workers. Together these panels turn performance work from speculation into measurement.

### Debugging technique

Reproduce the problem reliably before changing anything. Reduce the failing case to the smallest input that still fails, because a small case is both easier to reason about and a ready-made regression test. Form a hypothesis, test it with the cheapest possible experiment, and discard the explanation once evidence contradicts it rather than adding a second fix on top.

Read the error message and the stack trace from the top, not the bottom, and identify the first frame inside your own code. Check assumptions about inputs at the boundary of the failing function, because the defect is often upstream. When a bug resists, explain the code line by line to a listener or a rubber duck; the act of narrating forces the reasoning that silent staring skips.

The most common pitfalls are using the loose equality operator, confusing the value of this in nested callbacks, mutating shared arrays and objects, forgetting to await a promise, and treating a falsy value such as zero or the empty string as absent. Others are relying on the automatic semicolon insertion in risky line positions, confusing shallow and deep copies, and assuming that const makes an object immutable.

Many of these are prevented by tooling: strict equality lint rules, the no-floating-promises check, and a type checker that distinguishes optional from present values. The rest are prevented by conventions: prefer const, avoid mutation across module boundaries, use optional chaining only at genuine boundaries, and default with the nullish operator rather than the logical or when zero or empty string are valid.

### Code

`forEach` does not await, `sort` is lexicographic, and both look right:

```js
// WRONG: the loop returns before any promise resolves
[1, 2, 3].forEach(async (n) => { await save(n); });
console.log('this runs first');

// RIGHT
for (const n of [1, 2, 3]) await save(n);
await Promise.all([1, 2, 3].map(save));

// WRONG: string comparison
console.log([10, 9, 80].sort());              // [10, 80, 9]
// RIGHT
console.log([10, 9, 80].sort((a, b) => a - b));   // [9, 10, 80]
```

`this` is lost through a callback, and `NaN` is not equal to itself:

```js
const timer = {
  seconds: 0,
  startBroken() { setInterval(function () { this.seconds++; }, 1000); },
  startFixed() { setInterval(() => { this.seconds++; }, 1000); },
};

const x = NaN;
console.log(x === x);                 // false
console.log(Number.isNaN(x));         // true
console.log([1, NaN, 2].indexOf(NaN));      // -1
console.log([1, NaN, 2].includes(NaN));     // true
```

Immutability mistakes and the reference-comparison trap:

```js
const a = { list: [1, 2] };
const b = { ...a };            // shallow copy
b.list.push(3);
console.log(a.list);           // [1, 2, 3] — same nested array

// WRONG: distinct objects are never equal
// if (obj === { id: 1 }) { }
// RIGHT
const same = (x, y) => x?.id === y.id;

// WRONG: JSON round-trip loses Date, Map, Set, undefined and Infinity
// RIGHT: structuredClone
const original = { when: new Date(), tags: new Set(['a']) };
const clone = structuredClone(original);
console.log(clone.when instanceof Date, clone.tags instanceof Set);   // true true
```

### Idioms worth adopting

Guard clauses at the top of a function and early returns keep nesting shallow. Destructuring with defaults makes the data a function needs explicit at its signature. Deriving values instead of storing duplicates removes a whole class of synchronisation bugs. Small pure functions with names that state intent make the flow readable at a glance.

For collections, reach for map, filter, find and reduce before writing a manual loop, and switch to a plain loop when you need to break early or when the callback would obscure the logic. For asynchronous work, default to async and await with try-catch, use Promise.all for independent work, and attach an abort signal to anything that a user can cancel. These idioms make the common case simple and the failure case explicit.

### Readability and review

Code is read far more often than it is written. Name things for what they mean rather than how they are implemented, keep functions short enough to hold in mind, and let the formatter settle style so reviews discuss behaviour instead of whitespace. Comments should explain why a surprising decision was made, because the code already says what it does.

A good change is small, has a clear purpose, includes tests for the behaviour it adds, and does not mix refactoring with feature work. Reviews are most useful when they question design and edge cases rather than style. The test of a well-written module is that a new reader can predict what it does before running it, which is the goal of clarity over cleverness.

The platform defines several built-in error types, each signalling a category: Error for general failures, TypeError for a value of the wrong kind, RangeError for a value outside the allowed set, ReferenceError for a name that is not defined, SyntaxError for invalid code, and URIError for malformed encodings. Catching by type lets a handler react differently to a programming mistake than to an environmental failure such as a missing file.

A custom error class usually extends Error so it appears correctly in stack traces and can be distinguished with instanceof. Add a name property, a machine-readable code, and any structured context the caller needs. Error causes let you chain an underlying failure with a new one, so a high-level error can explain what the program was doing while preserving the original reason for debugging.

### Code

One base class plus a stable code is all a boundary handler needs:

```js
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, { code, status = 500, cause } = {}) {
    super(message, { cause });
    this.name = new.target.name;
    this.code = code ?? 'internal';
    this.status = status;
    Error.captureStackTrace?.(this, new.target);
  }
}

class NotFound extends AppError {
  constructor(what, id) { super(`${what} ${id} not found`, { code: 'not_found', status: 404 }); }
}
class ValidationFailed extends AppError {
  constructor(fields) { super('validation failed', { code: 'invalid', status: 422 }); this.fields = fields; }
}

try {
  throw new NotFound('order', 42);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(err instanceof AppError, err.code, err.status, err.message);
  // true not_found 404 order 42 not found
}
```

One handler maps every known error, and anything unknown becomes a safe 500:

```js
function toResponse(err) {
  if (err instanceof ValidationFailed) return { status: 422, body: { code: err.code, fields: err.fields } };
  if (err instanceof AppError) return { status: err.status, body: { code: err.code, message: err.message } };
  logError(err);                                   // full detail server-side only
  return { status: 500, body: { code: 'internal', message: 'something went wrong' } };
}
```

Errors as values for the expected cases, exceptions for the rest:

```js
const parseAge = (input) => {
  const n = Number(input);
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 0) return { ok: false, error: 'age must be a whole number >= 0' };
  return { ok: true, value: n };
};

const result = parseAge('x');
if (!result.ok) console.log(result.error);
else console.log(result.value);
```

Wrap at the boundary so the original stack survives: `new AppError('save failed', { cause: err })` keeps the cause chain readable without replacing the trace.

### Where to catch and where to throw

Throw at the point where an invariant is violated or input is invalid, and catch where there is something useful to do: recovering, retrying, adding context, or reporting to the user. A catch that only logs and continues is usually wrong, because it hides a failure from the logic that must handle it. Let unexpected errors propagate to a single boundary that turns them into a user-facing message and a log entry.

Distinguish operational errors such as a network timeout, which are expected and handled, from programmer errors such as passing the wrong type, which should fail loudly during development. Validate arguments at public boundaries, assert internal invariants, and prefer returning a result object over throwing when absence is an ordinary outcome, reserving exceptions for genuinely exceptional conditions.

### Global handlers and reporting

The window error event and the unhandledrejection event let a page react to failures that escaped every local handler, which is where a last-resort user message and a report to a monitoring service belong. In Node, the uncaughtException and unhandledRejection process events serve the same purpose, though the sound response to an uncaught exception in a server is usually to log and shut down rather than to continue in an unknown state.

Good error reports include the message, the stack, the user's action, the release version and relevant context, while excluding secrets. A stable identifier per error lets duplicates be grouped so the signal is not buried. Because stack traces are the primary debugging artifact, keep error objects intact rather than converting them to strings early, and pass them through layers unchanged.

JSON is a text format for structured data with objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans and null. JSON.parse turns text into a value and throws a syntax error on malformed input, so it must be guarded when the source is untrusted. The optional reviver function transforms values during parsing, which is how dates stored as strings are converted back into Date objects.

JSON.stringify turns a value into text, skipping undefined, functions and symbols in object properties and converting them to null inside arrays. A replacer function or array controls what is included, and the third argument indents the output for readability. A toJSON method on an object customises its serialisation, which is how Date and other built-ins produce sensible output.

### Code

The `replacer` and `reviver` are the two hooks, and they are how you round-trip types JSON does not know:

```js
const state = { when: new Date(), count: 0n, missing: undefined, fn: () => {} };

const json = JSON.stringify(state, (key, value) => {
  if (typeof value === 'bigint') return { __bigint: value.toString() };
  if (value instanceof Date) return { __date: value.toISOString() };
  if (typeof value === 'function' || value === undefined) return undefined;
  return value;
});
console.log(json);

const back = JSON.parse(json, (key, value) => {
  if (value && typeof value === 'object') {
    if ('__bigint' in value) return BigInt(value.__bigint);
    if ('__date' in value) return new Date(value.__date);
  }
  return value;
});
console.log(back.when instanceof Date, back.count + 1n);
```

The traps: `undefined` and functions vanish silently, `NaN` and `Infinity` become `null`, a cyclic object throws, and property order is insertion order for strings but numeric keys are sorted first:

```js
console.log(JSON.stringify({ b: 1, 2: 'x', a: 2 }));   // {"2":"x","b":1,"a":2}
console.log(JSON.stringify([1, undefined, NaN]));      // [1,null,null]
console.log(JSON.stringify({ a: 1 }, null, 2));        // pretty, 2 spaces

try { const c = {}; c.self = c; JSON.stringify(c); }
catch (err) { console.log(err.name); }                 // TypeError
```

Newline-delimited JSON is the format for streams: one value per line, constant memory, and a corrupt line can be skipped instead of discarding the file:

```js
const lines = data.split('\n').filter(Boolean);
let parsed = 0;
for (const line of lines) {
  try { const row = JSON.parse(line); parsed++; }
  catch { console.warn('skipping a bad line'); }
}
console.log(parsed, 'of', lines.length);
```

### Limits and safe usage

JSON has no representation for undefined, big integers, dates, maps, sets, regular expressions, functions or cyclic references; stringifying a cycle throws, and large integers lose precision. Attempting to use JSON as a general deep-clone mechanism therefore loses information silently, which is why structuredClone exists for that purpose. When a rich type must survive serialisation, encode it explicitly with a tag such as a type field and decode it on the way back.

Security matters on both sides. Never evaluate JSON with the language evaluator, because a crafted string can execute code; always use JSON.parse. When validating parsed input from an external source, treat every field as untrusted and check its type and range before use. A schema validator turns that checking into a single declarative step and produces clear errors instead of scattered conditionals.

A custom element is a class extending HTMLElement registered with customElements.define and used in markup as a hyphenated tag name. The browser calls lifecycle callbacks when the element is connected, disconnected or has an attribute changed, which gives a component its own place in the DOM with encapsulated state. Because it is a real element, it works with the normal DOM API, form submission and accessibility tooling.

The shadow DOM attaches a separate, encapsulated tree to an element so its internal styles and structure do not leak out and cannot be reached by ordinary selectors. This isolation is the core benefit of the platform component model and removes the need for a styling convention. Templates and slots let a component declare its structure once and project caller-provided content into named placeholders.

### Code

A custom element is a class plus a tag name with a hyphen, and the lifecycle hooks are `connected`, `disconnected` and `attributeChanged`:

```js
class StarRating extends HTMLElement {
  static observedAttributes = ['value', 'max'];
  #value = 0;

  constructor() {
    super();
    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
  }

  attributeChangedCallback(name, oldValue, newValue) {
    if (name === 'value') this.#value = Number(newValue) || 0;
    this.#render();
  }

  connectedCallback() { this.#render(); }

  #render() {
    const max = Number(this.getAttribute('max')) || 5;
    this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
      <style>:host { display: inline-flex; gap: 2px } span { color: #f59e0b }</style>
      <span>${'\u2605'.repeat(this.#value)}</span><span>${'\u2606'.repeat(max - this.#value)}</span>
    `;
  }
}

customElements.define('star-rating', StarRating);
// <star-rating value="3" max="5"></star-rating>
```

Dispatch events upward instead of calling out, so the component has no knowledge of its user:

```js
this.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('rating:change', {
  detail: { value: this.#value },
  bubbles: true,
  composed: true,          // composed crosses the shadow boundary
}));
```

A `<template>` in the document keeps the markup out of JavaScript and is cloned once per instance:

```html
<template id="row">
  <li class="row"><span class="name"></span><button>x</button></li>
</template>
```

```js
const tpl = document.getElementById('row');
const node = tpl.content.cloneNode(true);
node.querySelector('.name').textContent = user.name;
list.append(node);
```

Shadow DOM scopes styles in and blocks selectors from outside; tokens cross the boundary through CSS custom properties, which is the intended escape hatch.

### When to use the platform components

Web Components are strongest for reusable widgets that must work in any framework or none, such as a date picker or a chart published as a library. They interoperate with every framework because they are ordinary elements. They are weaker for application structure, where a framework's data flow and component composition offer more ergonomics than imperative DOM inside callbacks.

The trade-offs are ergonomic: the API is imperative and verbose, reactivity must be built by hand, and server rendering and accessibility require care. Adopting them is worthwhile when cross-framework reuse or strong style isolation is the goal. Otherwise, a framework component achieves the same result with less ceremony, and the two can coexist in one application.

WebAssembly is a compact binary format that runs at near-native speed in the same sandbox as JavaScript. It is compiled from languages such as C, C++, Rust or Go, and is loaded from JavaScript, which calls its exported functions and shares memory through an ArrayBuffer. This makes it the path for compute-heavy code such as codecs, simulation and existing native libraries.

WebAssembly complements rather than replaces JavaScript. It cannot touch the DOM directly, so JavaScript remains the orchestrator that loads modules, passes data and updates the interface. Crossing the boundary has a cost, so passing a pointer into shared memory is faster than passing many small values. For most applications the performance gain is unnecessary; adopt it when a profile shows a specific hot loop that dominates.

### Code

Instantiating a module and calling an export is a few lines, and the imports object is the only bridge to JavaScript:

```js
const bytes = await (await fetch('/math.wasm')).arrayBuffer();

const { instance } = await WebAssembly.instantiate(bytes, {
  env: {
    log: (n) => console.log('from wasm:', n),
    memory: new WebAssembly.Memory({ initial: 1 }),
  },
});

const { add, fill } = instance.exports;
console.log(add(2, 3));
fill(0, 10, 7);                       // write 7 into the first 10 bytes
const view = new Uint8Array(instance.exports.memory.buffer);
console.log([...view.slice(0, 5)]);
```

Passing strings across the boundary is the awkward part: you write bytes into the module's memory and pass an offset and a length.

```js
const encoder = new TextEncoder();
const encoded = encoder.encode('hello');
const ptr = instance.exports.alloc(encoded.length);
new Uint8Array(instance.exports.memory.buffer, ptr, encoded.length).set(encoded);
instance.exports.handle(ptr, encoded.length);
```

Canvas animation belongs in `requestAnimationFrame`, and the cheap win is to draw once per frame rather than per event:

```js
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
let t = 0;
function frame() {
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
  for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
    const a = t / 60 + i;
    const x = canvas.width / 2 + Math.cos(a) * (40 + i / 4);
    const y = canvas.height / 2 + Math.sin(a) * (40 + i / 4);
    ctx.fillStyle = `hsl(${(a * 57) % 360} 80% 60%)`;
    ctx.fillRect(x, y, 3, 3);
  }
  t++;
  requestAnimationFrame(frame);
}
requestAnimationFrame(frame);
```

For 3D, WebGL and WebGPU are the APIs; the pattern is always the same — create buffers once, upload attribute data, compile a shader pair, then issue draw calls per frame and keep per-frame allocation near zero.

### Canvas, WebGL and WebGPU

The canvas element provides a drawable surface with a two-dimensional context for shapes, text and images, or a three-dimensional context, WebGL, modelled on OpenGL for GPU-accelerated graphics. Canvas is used for charts, image editing, games and visualisation. Its contents are pixels, so accessibility requires a parallel textual representation, and drawing operations are imperative rather than declarative.

WebGPU is a newer graphics and compute API with a lower-overhead model than WebGL and explicit access to compute shaders, useful for rendering pipelines and general-purpose GPU work. Support is still spreading, so feature detection and a WebGL or canvas fallback are necessary. For most data visualisation, a higher-level library over canvas or SVG is the practical choice, and direct graphics programming is reserved for genuinely graphical applications.

A decorator annotates a class, method, field or accessor to modify or record something about it, written with an at sign before the declaration. Depending on their stage, decorators may wrap the declaration, receive metadata about it, or add behaviour such as logging, validation or dependency registration. They are widely used by frameworks for declarative wiring without hand-written boilerplate.

Decorator semantics have changed over several proposal stages, so behaviour depends on the toolchain configuration. They are convenient but obscure control flow, since a reader must know what each decorator does to understand the class. Use them where a framework expects them, and prefer explicit code elsewhere, because explicitness survives refactors and toolchain upgrades better than magic syntax.

### Code

Class decorators and method decorators receive the class or the method and return a replacement:

```js
function logged(original, context) {
  return function (...args) {
    console.log(`calling ${String(context.name)}(${args.length} args)`);
    const result = original.apply(this, args);
    console.log(`${String(context.name)} ->`, result);
    return result;
  };
}

class Cart {
  #items = [];
  @logged
  add(item) { this.#items.push(item); return this.#items.length; }
}

new Cart().add({ id: 1 });
// calling add(1 args)
// add -> 1
```

Decorators are still a proposal and the exact API has shifted, so keep them out of code you cannot easily change.

Stage-4 features that already work everywhere:

```js
const list = [3, 1, 2];
console.log(list.toSorted((a, b) => a - b), list);   // [1,2,3] [3,1,2]
console.log([1, 2, 3].toReversed());                 // [3, 2, 1]
console.log(Object.groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4], (n) => (n % 2 ? 'odd' : 'even')));
console.log([1, 2].with(0, 9));                      // [9, 2]
const { a, ...rest } = { a: 1, b: 2 };
console.log(rest);                                   // { b: 2 }
try { JSON.parse('{'); } catch { console.log('no binding needed'); }
```

The safest posture toward proposals is to feature-detect, not polyfill blindly:

```js
const groupBy = Object.groupBy ?? ((items, keyOf) => {
  const out = Object.create(null);
  for (const item of items) (out[keyOf(item)] ??= []).push(item);
  return out;
});
console.log(groupBy([1, 2], (n) => (n % 2 ? 'odd' : 'even')));
```

`using` and explicit resource management, `Temporal`, and pattern matching are the three to watch; each one removes a library rather than adding syntax you must learn.

### Reading proposals and shipping safely

New language features move through stages from an idea to a finished specification, and each stage carries a different stability guarantee. A feature at an early stage may change or be withdrawn, so shipping it in production ties the codebase to a specific toolchain. Check the compatibility data before adopting and plan for the day the syntax changes.

The safe workflow is to adopt a feature when it is widely supported in the engines you target, or when a transpiler can lower it faithfully. Prefer using features for readability inside a module rather than spreading an unstable syntax across the codebase. Keeping the toolchain current and the target browsers explicit turns this from guesswork into a maintenance routine.

Binding, borrowing and partial application are all the same mechanism:

```js
const base = { prefix: '>>', log(msg) { return `${this.prefix} ${msg}`; } };
const other = { prefix: '--' };
console.log(base.log('hi'));                 // >> hi
console.log(base.log.call(other, 'hi'));     // -- hi

const partial = (fn, ...preset) => (...rest) => fn(...preset, ...rest);
const warn = partial(base.log, 'WARN');
console.log(warn.call(base, 'disk full'));   // >> WARN disk full
```

`bind` is what makes a method safe to pass as a callback, and arrow functions are the other option:

```js
class Timer {
  ticks = 0;
  start() {
    this.handle = this.tick.bind(this);      // stable reference, needed to clear
    this.id = setInterval(this.handle, 1000);
  }
  tick() { this.ticks++; }
  stop() { clearInterval(this.id); }
}
```

Closures over `let` create a fresh binding per iteration; closures over `var` share one:

```js
const make = (n) => () => n;
console.log([1, 2, 3].map(make).map((f) => f()));    // [1, 2, 3]

const counters = [];
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  counters.push({ get: () => i, set: (v) => { i = v; } });
}
counters[1].set(99);
console.log(counters.map((c) => c.get()));           // [0, 99, 2]
```

### this and binding recipes

The four call forms determine this: a plain call gives undefined in strict mode, a method call gives the receiver, a call with new gives the fresh object, and an arrow inherits from its enclosing scope. The bug appears when a method is detached from its receiver, such as passing obj.save to a scheduler; the fix is obj.save.bind(obj) or a wrapper arrow.

A class field arrow binds once per instance: class Timer { tick = () => { this.count++; }; }. Because the field is initialised in the constructor, the arrow already closes over the instance, so tick can be passed anywhere. The alternative, binding in the constructor, achieves the same result explicitly and is still common in older codebases.

call and apply borrow a method with a different receiver: Array.prototype.slice.call(arrayLike) converted array-like values before spread existed, and Math.max.apply(null, numbers) spread an array into arguments before Math.max accepted spread. Both are now written as Array.from(arrayLike) and Math.max(...numbers), which are clearer and faster.

Partial application fixes leading arguments: const double = multiply.bind(null, 2). Currying instead produces a chain of single-argument functions, and both are useful for building specialised functions from general ones. A named wrapper such as function double(n) { return multiply(2, n); } is often more readable than either and should be preferred when the function is used more than once.

### Function shapes, arity and metaprogramming

A function's length property reports the number of parameters before the first default or rest, which is a weak signal but is used by some libraries to decide how to invoke a callback. Relying on it is fragile; an explicit options object avoids the question entirely.

Higher-order functions that wrap others, called decorators or combinators, are the backbone of composition. A logging wrapper, a retry wrapper, a timing wrapper and a validation wrapper all share the shape: they accept a function and return a new function with the same contract. Writing them once and reusing them beats repeating the concern at every call site.

Tail calls are not generally optimised in JavaScript, so deep recursion throws a stack overflow. Convert deep recursion to a loop, or to an explicit stack when traversing a tree. Where recursion is the clearest expression and the depth is bounded by the data, such as a small directory tree, it is fine to keep.

Argument validation at the boundary keeps the interior simple. A small assert helper that throws a TypeError with the parameter name turns a confusing downstream failure into a precise message, and it disappears from production builds if the bundler strips the checks.

Class fields live on the instance; prototype methods are shared, and `super` looks them up on the prototype chain rather than on the instance:

```js
class Base {
  kind = 'base';
  describe() { return `kind=${this.kind}`; }
}
class Child extends Base {
  kind = 'child';
  describe() { return `child of ${super.describe()}`; }
}
const c = new Child();
console.log(c.describe());                    // child of kind=child
console.log(Object.hasOwn(c, 'kind'));        // true — a field, not a method
console.log(Object.hasOwn(c, 'describe'));    // false — inherited
```

Arrow fields capture the instance, which is why they work as event handlers and methods are often the wrong choice there:

```js
class Button {
  label = 'save';
  onClick = () => this.label;                 // bound to the instance
  onClickMethod() { return this.label; }      // depends on the receiver
}
const b = new Button();
const { onClick, onClickMethod } = b;
console.log(onClick());                       // 'save'
console.log(onClickMethod());                 // TypeError: this is undefined
```

Class identity is per definition, so hot-reloading two definitions creates two incompatible types:

```js
const A = class {};
console.log(new A() instanceof A);            // true
console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(A.prototype) === Object.prototype);   // true
console.log(A.name);                          // 'A' for a named class expression
```

### Prototypes versus classes in real code

Before class syntax, inheritance was written by linking prototypes: function Animal(name) { this.name = name; } Animal.prototype.speak = function () { return this.name; }; function Dog(name) { Animal.call(this, name); } Dog.prototype = Object.create(Animal.prototype); Dog.prototype.constructor = Dog;. Reading this pattern is still necessary because a great deal of library code predates classes, and the class syntax compiles down to exactly these steps.

The class form is the same mechanism with clearer syntax: class Dog extends Animal { speak() { return super.speak() + ' woof'; } }. What changes is not semantics but readability, plus the guarantees that a class cannot be called without new and that derived constructors must call super before touching this.

Mixing behaviour without a shared base uses functions that copy methods onto a prototype: function withTimestamp(Base) { return class extends Base { stamp() { return Date.now(); } }; }. This is a mixin, and it composes better than deep hierarchies because each concern is independent and can be applied in any order.

Composition is usually the better default. A class that holds a logger, a repository and a cache and delegates to them is easier to test than one that inherits those behaviours, because each collaborator can be replaced independently. Reach for inheritance when there is a genuine is-a relationship and the base class was designed for extension.

### Encapsulation and immutability patterns

Private class fields with a leading hash are the platform's real encapsulation: class Wallet { #balance = 0; deposit(n) { this.#balance += n; } }. The field is unreachable from outside the class, even through bracket access, and it cannot be shadowed by a subclass.

Closures provide encapsulation without classes: function wallet(initial = 0) { let balance = initial; return { deposit: (n) => (balance += n), read: () => balance }; }. The returned object exposes behaviour but not the variable, which is the module pattern in miniature and predates private fields.

A WeakMap as a side table attaches private data to objects you do not control: const data = new WeakMap(); function attach(obj, value) { data.set(obj, value); }. The entry disappears when the object is collected, so nothing leaks, and the private value never appears as an enumerable property that serialisation or iteration would expose.

For immutability, return new objects from updates: function rename(user, name) { return { ...user, name }; }. Freeze shared configuration with Object.freeze to catch accidental writes in strict mode, and reserve deep freezing for values that are genuinely constant, because freezing has a cost and blocks extension.

Sequential work awaits in order: const a = await first(); const b = await second(a);. Parallel work starts everything and collects: const [a, b] = await Promise.all([first(), second()]). Bounded parallelism processes many items a few at a time, which is the pattern for thousands of requests against a server you must not overwhelm.

A timeout is a race between the real work and a rejecting timer: const timeout = (ms) => new Promise((_, reject) => setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('timeout')), ms)); await Promise.race([fetchData(), timeout(5000)]). The timer keeps the process alive unless it is cleared, so a careful version stores the identifier and clears it in a finally block.

allSettled is the correct tool when partial success is acceptable: const results = await Promise.allSettled(urls.map(fetchUrl)); const ok = results.filter((r) => r.status === 'fulfilled').map((r) => r.value). One failure no longer discards every successful result, which makes bulk operations resilient.

Promise.any returns the first success and rejects only when all fail, which fits redundant sources: await Promise.any([primary(), fallback()]). The rejection reason is an AggregateError carrying every failure, so logging it shows why each source was unusable rather than only the last one.

### Code

The exact interleaving of sync, micro and macro work, including the second-tick surprise:

```js
setTimeout(() => console.log('A: macrotask'), 0);
queueMicrotask(() => console.log('B: microtask'));
(async () => {
  console.log('C: async body start');
  await null;
  console.log('D: after await');
})();
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('E: then'));
console.log('F: sync end');

// C: async body start
// F: sync end
// B: microtask
// D: after await
// E: then
// A: macrotask
```

Because microtasks drain fully, a self-scheduling microtask starves timers and the UI:

```js
let n = 0;
const spinMicro = () => {
  if (n++ < 5) queueMicrotask(spinMicro);          // safe, bounded
};
setTimeout(() => console.log('timer'), 0);
spinMicro();
console.log('sync done');

// unbounded version (do not ship): queueMicrotask(function again() { queueMicrotask(again); });
```

Awaiting inside a loop and parallelising the same work:

```js
const delay = (ms, v) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms));

const t0 = performance.now();
const seq = [];
for (const ms of [40, 40, 40]) seq.push(await delay(ms, ms));
console.log('sequential', Math.round(performance.now() - t0), seq);   // ~120

const t1 = performance.now();
const par = await Promise.all([delay(40, 1), delay(40, 2), delay(40, 3)]);
console.log('parallel', Math.round(performance.now() - t1), par);      // ~40
```

Named exports are live bindings; a default export is a snapshot of a value:

```js
// state.js
let current = 0;
export const read = () => current;
export const bump = () => { current += 1; };
export default current;      // captured at evaluation time

// main.js
import snapshot, { read, bump } from './state.js';
console.log(read(), snapshot);   // 0 0
bump();
bump();
console.log(read(), snapshot);   // 2 0 — the binding moved, the snapshot did not
```

Circular imports work only because bindings are resolved lazily; a value used at module top level will be undefined:

```js
// a.js
export const name = 'a';
import { describe } from './b.js';
console.log('a sees:', describe);            // undefined at this point

// b.js
import { name } from './a.js';
export const describe = `b from ${name}`;    // fine: evaluated after a.js's name exists
export function later() { return `b from ${name}`; }
```

The barrel file is convenient and expensive; import the module you need when it matters:

```js
// index.js — pulls in every module for one import
export * from './parser.js';
export * from './renderer.js';

// preferred in hot paths
import { parse } from './parser.js';

// lazy, when the module is large or conditional
const { render } = await import('./renderer.js');
```

### Dynamic import and lazy loading

Dynamic import returns a promise and can take a computed specifier: const { parse } = await import('./parsers/csv.js'). A bundler detects the call and emits a separate chunk, so the code is fetched only when the branch runs, which is the mechanism behind lazy routes and optional features.

A practical use is to load a heavy dependency on demand: async function exportPdf(rows) { const { makePdf } = await import('./pdf.js'); return makePdf(rows); }. Users who never export do not pay for the library, and the main bundle stays small. The cost is an extra request on first use, which is a good trade for a feature used by a minority of sessions.

Lazy loading a route lets the initial page render without the code for pages the user has not visited: const Page = lazy(() => import('./Page.js')). Combined with a suspense boundary that shows a placeholder, the interface stays responsive while the chunk downloads. Prefetching the likely next chunk on hover or idle time removes the wait without blocking anything.

Because imported modules are cached, a dynamic import in a hot path resolves immediately after the first call, but the first call still awaits a microtask. Code that must be synchronous, such as a module-level initialiser that cannot be async, cannot use dynamic import and must use a static import instead.

### Testability and dependency seams

Imports are captured at module evaluation, so a test cannot easily replace a directly imported function. The fix is to combine imports with injection: import { send } from './mail.js'; export function makeNotifier(mailer = send) { return (msg) => mailer(msg); }. Production uses the default, and the test passes a spy, so no global patching is required.

Keeping side effects out of module scope makes modules importable in a test or a tool without triggering network calls or file writes. A module that only defines functions and values is trivially importable; one that connects to a database on import forces every importer to pay that cost and makes the module impossible to test in isolation.

An entry point should wire the pieces together and do almost nothing else. Composition at the edge, where concrete implementations are chosen and passed inward, keeps the core logic free of environment assumptions. This is the same idea as a seam in testing, applied at application scale.

Circular imports, module-scope side effects and hidden singletons are the three patterns that most often make a codebase hard to test. Recognising them early and preferring explicit parameters over ambient state keeps the module graph acyclic and the behaviour predictable.

Read-then-write batching is the difference between one layout and hundreds:

```js
const rows = [...document.querySelectorAll('.row')];

// read all first
const widths = rows.map((r) => r.getBoundingClientRect().width);
const tallest = Math.max(...rows.map((r) => r.offsetHeight));

// then write all
rows.forEach((r, i) => {
  r.style.width = `${Math.max(widths[i], 120)}px`;
  r.style.height = `${tallest}px`;
});
```

Delegation plus `closest` keeps one listener working for any number of future rows:

```js
const table = document.querySelector('#grid');
table.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  const cell = event.target.closest('td[data-id]');
  if (!cell) return;
  table.querySelectorAll('td.is-selected').forEach((td) => td.classList.remove('is-selected'));
  cell.classList.add('is-selected');
  table.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cell:pick', { detail: { id: cell.dataset.id }, bubbles: true }));
});
```

The listener options that prevent the two classic performance bugs:

```js
// passive: the listener promises never to call preventDefault, so scrolling is not blocked
window.addEventListener('touchstart', onTouch, { passive: true });

// once: removed automatically, which avoids a manual guard flag
document.querySelector('#accept').addEventListener('click', onAccept, { once: true });

// signal: one controller removes a whole group at once
const controller = new AbortController();
for (const el of document.querySelectorAll('.draggable')) {
  el.addEventListener('pointerdown', onDown, { signal: controller.signal });
}
controller.abort();
```

### Event handling recipes

Delegation handles an unbounded list with one listener: list.addEventListener('click', (e) => { const button = e.target.closest('button[data-action]'); if (!button) return; handle(button.dataset.action, button.dataset.id); }). Because the listener lives on the container, items added later are handled automatically and no listeners need to be removed when rows are recycled.

Debouncing keeps a search field responsive: const onInput = debounce((value) => runSearch(value), 250); input.addEventListener('input', (e) => onInput(e.target.value)). Throttling instead limits a scroll handler to a fixed rate, which is the right choice when intermediate values matter, such as updating a progress indicator while scrolling.

Passive listeners let the browser scroll without waiting for the handler: window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true }). A non-passive touch or wheel listener that never calls preventDefault blocks the compositor and makes scrolling feel heavy, so passive should be the default for handlers that only observe.

Custom events decouple a component from its surroundings: element.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('selection', { detail: { id }, bubbles: true })). A parent listens for selection rather than reaching into the child, and the child knows nothing about the parent, which is what allows the two to be developed and tested independently.

### Forms, validation and accessibility

Read form values from FormData rather than from individual inputs: const data = Object.fromEntries(new FormData(form)). This captures checkboxes, selects and multi-value fields with the same code and avoids forgetting a field after markup changes. Convert and validate each value immediately, because every field arrives as a string.

Client validation improves feedback and must never replace server validation, because the client is fully under the user's control. Validate on submit and on blur rather than on every keystroke, so the user is not scolded mid-typing, and place the message next to the field with aria-describedby linking them so a screen reader announces it.

Accessibility is a correctness requirement. Use real buttons rather than clickable divs, label every input, keep a logical tab order, and never remove focus outlines without replacing them with a visible focus style. Keyboard users must be able to reach and operate everything a mouse user can.

Do not put heavy synchronous work in a submit handler, because it freezes the interface and the user may click again. Disable the submit button while the request is in flight, show a progress state, and re-enable it in a finally block so a failure does not leave the form permanently disabled.

Conditional requests turn a large response into a small 304, and the ETag is the key:

```js
let etag = localStorage.getItem('items:etag') ?? '';
const res = await fetch('/api/items', { headers: etag ? { 'if-none-match': etag } : {} });

if (res.status === 304) {
  console.log('unchanged, using cache');
} else if (res.ok) {
  const data = await res.json();
  etag = res.headers.get('etag') ?? '';
  localStorage.setItem('items', JSON.stringify(data));
  localStorage.setItem('items:etag', etag);
}
```

A stale-while-revalidate cache in twenty lines, with a versioned key so a schema change cannot serve old shapes:

```js
const KEY = 'items:v2';

async function loadItems({ maxAgeMs = 60_000 } = {}) {
  const raw = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
  const cached = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : null;
  const fresh = cached && Date.now() - cached.at < maxAgeMs;
  if (fresh) return cached.value;

  const value = await fetch('/api/items').then((r) => r.json());
  localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify({ at: Date.now(), value }));
  return value;
}
```

Retry only the failures worth retrying, and bound the waiting:

```js
const RETRY = new Set([408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504]);

async function get(url, attempts = 3) {
  for (let i = 1; ; i++) {
    const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(8000) });
    if (res.ok) return res.json();
    if (i >= attempts || !RETRY.has(res.status)) {
      throw new Error(`${res.status} ${res.statusText}`);
    }
    const wait = Math.min(2000, 200 * 2 ** (i - 1)) * (0.5 + Math.random() / 2);
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, wait));
  }
}
```

### Caching and offline behaviour

A simple in-memory cache keyed by request avoids duplicate calls during one session: const cache = new Map(); const get = (url) => cache.has(url) ? cache.get(url) : (cache.set(url, getJson(url)), cache.get(url)). Always bound such a cache or expire entries, otherwise a long session accumulates responses indefinitely and holds memory that is never released.

Persisting a cache to storage makes a second visit instant: write the parsed response to IndexedDB with a timestamp, read it on load, and revalidate in the background. Showing cached data immediately and replacing it when the network responds is called stale-while-revalidate and gives the impression of an offline-capable application without any special infrastructure.

A service worker intercepts requests and can serve a cached shell when the network fails. The strategy matters: cache-first for immutable assets with hashed filenames, network-first for data that must be fresh, and a fallback page for navigation requests. Version the cache name and delete old caches on activation, otherwise users see stale builds forever.

Storage quotas differ across browsers and can be evicted under pressure, so a cache is an optimisation and never the system of record. Only user-authored data deserves a durable home, and even that should be synchronised to a server if losing it would be unacceptable. Treat anything on the client as disposable.

### Choosing the right storage

The decision follows size, lifetime and sensitivity. A cookie is for a session token the server must receive. sessionStorage is for ephemeral UI state that should vanish with the tab. localStorage is for small preferences. IndexedDB is for structured data, files and caches that exceed a few megabytes. In-memory is for anything that need not survive a reload.

Never store secrets in localStorage or sessionStorage, because any script on the origin can read them and a single cross-site scripting flaw exposes every user. Authentication is best carried by an httpOnly, secure, same-site cookie that JavaScript cannot read, which removes the token from the reach of injected code entirely.

Values in web storage are strings, so objects must be serialised. Guard the parse, because the stored value can be corrupted or written by an older version of the application: function readJson(key, fallback) { try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(key) ?? '') ?? fallback; } catch { return fallback; } }. A missing try-catch here turns a small corruption into a permanently broken page.

IndexedDB upgrades are versioned, so schema changes need a migration path. Open with an incremented version, and in the upgrade callback create or alter stores and indexes. Because the upgrade runs only when the stored version is lower, the code must handle a fresh database and every previous version, which is the same discipline a server-side database migration requires.

