### Map, Set and WeakMap in depth

Map has get, set, has, delete, clear, size and an ordered iterator over entries, keys or values. It accepts any value as a key, including NaN, which it matches to itself unlike the SameValueZero comparison used elsewhere; -0 and +0 are the same key. Keys are compared by identity for objects, so two structurally identical objects are two different keys. Map is a proper iterable, so it spreads, destructures and works with for-of directly, and constructing from an array of pairs gives you a conversion from a plain record.

Set stores unique values with the same key comparison rules and exposes add, has, delete, clear, size and ordered iteration. The useful patterns are deduplication, membership, and set algebra that you write yourself because the standard library does not provide union, intersection or difference. Building those as small functions over Sets is straightforward: union by spreading into a new Set, intersection by filtering one Set against has on the other, difference by filtering with the negation. They are linear rather than the quadratic version that filters arrays against arrays.

WeakMap and WeakSet hold keys weakly, so an entry disappears once the key is otherwise unreachable and the garbage collector runs. This is the mechanism for attaching metadata to an object without preventing its collection, which is exactly what the classic pattern of adding a hidden property does and why that pattern leaks. Both are non-iterable and have no size, because the contents can change at any moment as the collector runs; a size that changes asynchronously is not something you can reason about, so the API deliberately omits it.

The standard use for a WeakMap is private state. A module-level WeakMap from an instance to a record of its private fields gives genuine encapsulation without a property that reflection can find, and the entry is released with the instance. It is also how a library memoises per-object: a WeakMap from the object to its computed result, so the result lives exactly as long as the object does. A regular Map for the same purpose is a leak, because the key is retained forever and the map grows with every distinct object ever seen.

The Map versus object decision has a performance edge worth knowing. An object with a fixed shape is faster to read and write than a Map because the engine can use an inline cache keyed on the property's position; a Map has a method call and a lookup. Conversely, a Map with many keys, non-string keys, frequent additions and deletions, or a need for ordering is faster and clearer than an object whose shape is constantly changing, since every new key on an object invalidates the shape and can force the engine to rebuild the hidden class.

Converting between the two is a common boundary operation. Object.fromEntries(map) turns a Map of string keys into a plain object, and new Map(Object.entries(obj)) does the reverse. Note that Object.fromEntries only keeps string and symbol keys, so a Map with object keys loses information silently when converted. A convenient helper is a Next-style custom type with a nullable internal map, because representing an absent value with null rather than undefined catches the case where the caller forgot to check, and TypeScript then forces the check.

### Custom keys and collision handling

When an object must be a key by content rather than by identity, derive a canonical string. The recipe is to pick the identifying fields, sort them, encode each as name and value with a separator that cannot appear in the values, and join. Using JSON.stringify of a sorted field list is the common shortcut and it is acceptable for simple values, but it is order-sensitive unless you sort the keys, it cannot distinguish a missing field from an undefined one in some encodings, and its output can be ambiguous for values that contain the separators.

A better approach is an explicit encode function that escapes the delimiters. For fields joined by the unit separator character, replace occurrences of that character and of the escape character in each value first, then join. This is the same principle as CSV quoting. The function should be pure and documented, because its output is now an implicit cache key or database key and a change to it invalidates every stored key. Versioning the encoding, perhaps by prefixing a schema version, makes that migration explicit instead of silent.

Numeric composite keys are a faster option when the components are bounded. Pack the fields into a single number with bit shifts and masks, which is exact while the total width stays within the safe integer range, and falls back to BigInt beyond it. Packing gives a key that compares and sorts numerically and hashes cheaply, and it is the standard technique for grid coordinates, tile identifiers and spatial buckets. The cost in readability is real, so keep the pack and unpack functions adjacent and unit-tested.

A binary search tree gives ordered traversal for free, provided you never recurse deeper than the stack allows:

```js
class BST {
  #root = null;
  insert(value) {
    const node = { value, left: null, right: null };
    if (!this.#root) { this.#root = node; return; }
    for (let cur = this.#root; ; ) {
      if (value < cur.value) { if (!cur.left) { cur.left = node; return; } cur = cur.left; }
      else { if (!cur.right) { cur.right = node; return; } cur = cur.right; }
    }
  }
  *inOrder() {
    const stack = [];
    for (let cur = this.#root; cur || stack.length; ) {
      while (cur) { stack.push(cur); cur = cur.left; }
      cur = stack.pop(); yield cur.value; cur = cur.right;
    }
  }
  has(value) {
    for (let cur = this.#root; cur; ) {
      if (value === cur.value) return true;
      cur = value < cur.value ? cur.left : cur.right;
    }
    return false;
  }
  height() {
    const walk = (node) => (node ? 1 + Math.max(walk(node.left), walk(node.right)) : 0);
    return walk(this.#root);
  }
}
const tree = new BST();
for (const v of [5, 3, 8, 1, 4, 7, 9]) tree.insert(v);
console.log([...tree.inOrder()], tree.has(4), tree.height());   // [1,3,4,5,7,8,9] true 3
```

A binary heap is the array-backed priority queue behind schedulers; sift-up and sift-down keep the invariant:

```js
class MinHeap {
  #a = [];
  get size() { return this.#a.length; }
  push(value) {
    const a = this.#a; a.push(value);
    for (let i = a.length - 1; i > 0; ) {
      const p = (i - 1) >> 1;
      if (a[p] <= a[i]) break;
      [a[p], a[i]] = [a[i], a[p]]; i = p;
    }
  }
  pop() {
    const a = this.#a;
    if (!a.length) return undefined;
    const top = a[0], last = a.pop();
    if (a.length) {
      a[0] = last;
      for (let i = 0; ; ) {
        const l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1;
        let smallest = i;
        if (l < a.length && a[l] < a[smallest]) smallest = l;
        if (r < a.length && a[r] < a[smallest]) smallest = r;
        if (smallest === i) break;
        [a[i], a[smallest]] = [a[smallest], a[i]]; i = smallest;
      }
    }
    return top;
  }
}
const heap = new MinHeap();
for (const v of [5, 1, 8, 3]) heap.push(v);
const out = []; while (heap.size) out.push(heap.pop());
console.log(out);   // [ 1, 3, 5, 8 ]
```

With a heap, heap sort and the classic `n` smallest problem are a few lines:

```js
const nSmallest = (values, n) => {
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  for (const value of values) heap.push(value);
  return Array.from({ length: n }, () => heap.pop());
};
console.log(nSmallest([9, 4, 7, 1, 8, 2], 3));   // [ 1, 2, 4 ]
```

A trie turns prefix search into a walk, which is how autocomplete stays fast as the word list grows:

```js
class Trie {
  #root = {};
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.#root;
    for (const ch of word) node = node[ch] ??= {};
    node['$'] = true;
  }
  #walk(prefix) {
    let node = this.#root;
    for (const ch of prefix) { node = node[ch]; if (!node) return null; }
    return node;
  }
  has(word) { return Boolean(this.#walk(word)?.['$']); }
  withPrefix(prefix) {
    const start = this.#walk(prefix);
    const out = [];
    const visit = (node, path) => {
      if (node['$']) out.push(path);
      for (const [ch, child] of Object.entries(node)) if (ch !== '$') visit(child, path + ch);
    };
    if (start) visit(start, prefix);
    return out;
  }
}
const trie = new Trie();
for (const word of ['car', 'care', 'cart', 'core']) trie.insert(word);
console.log(trie.withPrefix('car'), trie.has('care'));   // [ 'car', 'care', 'cart' ] true
```

### Heaps and priority queues

A binary heap is a complete binary tree stored in an array, where every parent is smaller than or equal to its children in a min-heap. Because it is complete, the array representation is exact and no pointers are needed: for the element at index i, its children are at two i plus one and two i plus two, and its parent is at the floor of i minus one divided by two. Peek is constant time, and insert and remove-min are logarithmic because a single path from the leaf or root is walked while swapping.

Sift-up and sift-down are the only two operations. Insert appends at the end and sifts up while the parent is larger, swapping each time. Remove-min replaces the root with the last element, shrinks the array and sifts down while a child is smaller, swapping with the smaller child so the invariant is restored along one path only. The choice of the smaller child is what makes sift-down correct; swapping with the larger one produces a structure that is not a heap and that fails only for particular shapes.

Building a heap from an existing array is linear, not linearithmic, if you sift down from the last non-leaf back to the root rather than inserting one element at a time. This is the basis of heapsort, which is in-place and O(n log n) in the worst case but has poor cache behaviour compared with quicksort and is not stable. In practice heapsort's real value is as the safety net in a hybrid sort that switches to it when quicksort's recursion depth grows too large, which is how most library sorts avoid the quadratic worst case.

A priority queue is a heap with the operations named for the domain: enqueue with a priority, dequeue the highest priority, peek the highest priority. The comparator decides what highest means, and passing the comparator in rather than baking in an ordering is what makes the structure reusable. A stable priority queue also records insertion order and breaks ties by that, which matters when equal priorities should be processed in arrival order — a scheduler that does not do this reorders equal-priority jobs arbitrarily.

The classic applications are scheduling and streaming. Dijkstra's and A* search both maintain a frontier ordered by tentative cost. A k-way merge of sorted inputs uses a heap of the current head of each input. A streaming median uses two heaps, a max-heap for the lower half and a min-heap for the upper, rebalanced after every insertion so their sizes differ by at most one; the median is then the top of one or the average of both tops. Top-k is a heap of size k with the smallest of the retained elements at the root.

Implementing it in JavaScript means writing the comparator carefully. A comparator that returns a boolean instead of a number is the most common defect: the sift loop tests a relational expression, which coerces, and the result is a heap that is subtly wrong for some inputs and correct for others. Return the difference or the result of the comparison operators, and unit-test with a random shuffle followed by repeated remove-min to verify the output is sorted. That test catches essentially every insertion, removal and comparator bug in one stroke.

### Tries and prefix structures

A trie stores strings character by character along edges, with a node per prefix. Lookup, insert and delete run in time proportional to the length of the string, independent of how many strings are stored, which is what distinguishes it from a hash table where a longer key costs more to hash and where prefix queries are impossible. The price is memory: one node per distinct prefix, which for many short strings with few shared prefixes can be far more than the strings themselves.

Each node either stores a map from the next character to the child and a flag marking the end of a word, or uses a fixed 26-slot array for lowercase ASCII. The map version uses less memory for sparse alphabets and supports arbitrary Unicode; the array version is faster and is appropriate when the alphabet is known to be small. Marking the end of a word is essential and is separate from having children, because a word can be a prefix of another and both are stored in the same path.

Prefix search is the operation a trie exists for. Walk the prefix, then collect all words below that node with a depth-first traversal. Autocomplete, spell-check candidate generation, IP routing tables with longest-prefix match, and dictionary word games all reduce to this. The collect step should take a limit so a query for the empty prefix does not traverse the entire dictionary, and should terminate early once the limit is reached rather than building the full list and slicing it.

An adjacency list plus breadth-first search finds the shortest number of hops, while depth-first search answers reachability:

```js
const graph = new Map([
  ['a', ['b', 'c']], ['b', ['d']], ['c', ['d', 'e']], ['d', ['e']], ['e', []],
]);

function bfs(start, goal) {
  const queue = [[start]];
  const seen = new Set([start]);
  while (queue.length) {
    const path = queue.shift();
    if (path.at(-1) === goal) return path;
    for (const next of graph.get(path.at(-1)) ?? []) {
      if (!seen.has(next)) { seen.add(next); queue.push([...path, next]); }
    }
  }
  return null;
}
console.log(bfs('a', 'e'));   // [ 'a', 'c', 'e' ]

function dfs(node, seen = new Set()) {
  seen.add(node);
  for (const next of graph.get(node) ?? []) if (!seen.has(next)) dfs(next, seen);
  return seen;
}
console.log([...dfs('a')].sort());   // [ 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e' ]
```

A topological order exists only without cycles, and consuming in-degrees proves both at once:

```js
function topo(nodes, edges) {
  const indegree = new Map(nodes.map((n) => [n, 0]));
  const out = new Map(nodes.map((n) => [n, []]));
  for (const [from, to] of edges) { out.get(from).push(to); indegree.set(to, indegree.get(to) + 1); }
  const ready = nodes.filter((n) => indegree.get(n) === 0);
  const order = [];
  while (ready.length) {
    const node = ready.shift(); order.push(node);
    for (const next of out.get(node)) {
      indegree.set(next, indegree.get(next) - 1);
      if (indegree.get(next) === 0) ready.push(next);
    }
  }
  return order.length === nodes.length ? order : null;   // null means a cycle
}
console.log(topo(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], [['a', 'b'], ['a', 'c'], ['b', 'd'], ['c', 'd']]));
```

Dijkstra relaxes each edge only when it finds a strictly shorter route to a node:

```js
function dijkstra(adj, start) {
  const dist = new Map([...adj.keys()].map((node) => [node, Infinity]));
  dist.set(start, 0);
  const queue = [[0, start]];
  while (queue.length) {
    queue.sort((x, y) => x[0] - y[0]);
    const [d, node] = queue.shift();
    if (d > dist.get(node)) continue;
    for (const [next, weight] of adj.get(node)) {
      const nd = d + weight;
      if (nd < dist.get(next)) { dist.set(next, nd); queue.push([nd, next]); }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
console.log(dijkstra(new Map([
  ['a', [['b', 1], ['c', 4]]], ['b', [['c', 2], ['d', 5]]], ['c', [['d', 1]]], ['d', []],
]), 'a'));
```

Sorting: merge sort is stable and predictable, quick sort is fast in practice once the pivot avoids the worst case:

```js
function mergeSort(a) {
  if (a.length < 2) return a;
  const mid = a.length >> 1;
  const left = mergeSort(a.slice(0, mid)), right = mergeSort(a.slice(mid));
  const out = [];
  while (left.length && right.length) out.push(left[0] <= right[0] ? left.shift() : right.shift());
  return [...out, ...left, ...right];
}
console.log(mergeSort([3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6]));   // [ 1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9 ]

function quickSort(a) {
  if (a.length < 2) return a;
  const pivot = a[a.length >> 1];
  const less = a.filter((x) => x < pivot), eq = a.filter((x) => x === pivot), more = a.filter((x) => x > pivot);
  return [...quickSort(less), ...eq, ...quickSort(more)];
}
console.log(quickSort([3, 1, 4, 1, 5]));   // [ 1, 1, 3, 4, 5 ]
```

Binary search depends only on the sorted invariant, and getting the midpoint and bounds right is the whole trick:

```js
function binarySearch(sorted, target) {
  let lo = 0, hi = sorted.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
    if (sorted[mid] === target) return mid;
    if (sorted[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid - 1;
  }
  return -1;
}
function lowerBound(sorted, target) {
  let lo = 0, hi = sorted.length;
  while (lo < hi) {
    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
    if (sorted[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid;
  }
  return lo;   // first index that is >= target
}
const sorted = [1, 3, 3, 5, 8];
console.log(binarySearch(sorted, 5), lowerBound(sorted, 3));   // 3 1
```

### Traversal and shortest paths

Breadth-first search visits a graph in order of distance from the source when every edge has the same weight. It uses a queue, marks a vertex when it is enqueued rather than when it is dequeued, and therefore finds the shortest number of edges to every reachable vertex. Marking on dequeue instead of enqueue is the classic bug: it lets a vertex be enqueued several times, which turns a linear traversal into something much worse and can duplicate work exponentially on a dense graph.

Depth-first search uses a stack, either explicitly or through recursion, and is the right tool for connectivity, cycle detection, topological ordering and strongly connected components. A colour marking with white, grey and black states distinguishes an unexplored vertex from one on the current path from one fully explored; an edge to a grey vertex is a back edge and therefore a cycle. In an undirected graph a back edge means a cycle, and in a directed graph it means a cycle too, which is the basis of cycle detection in a dependency graph.

Topological sort orders a directed acyclic graph so that every edge points forward. Kahn's algorithm repeatedly removes a vertex with no incoming edges, which also detects a cycle because a non-empty graph with no such vertex contains one. The depth-first variant emits vertices in reverse post-order. Build systems, task schedulers, migration orderings and package installation all use this, and the required companion output is which vertices were left over when a cycle was detected, because that set is the deadlock to break.

Dijkstra's algorithm finds shortest paths with non-negative weights by repeatedly extracting the nearest unsettled vertex from a priority queue and relaxing its outgoing edges. With a binary heap it is linearithmic in the edge count, which is good enough for almost every application. Without a priority queue it degrades to quadratic, which is fine only for tiny graphs. The relaxation step must compare the newly computed distance with the stored one and update only when it is smaller, and the stored distance for an unreached vertex should be Infinity so the comparison works without a special case.

A* adds a heuristic that estimates the remaining cost to the goal and orders the frontier by the sum of the known cost and the estimate. With an admissible heuristic that never overestimates, A* finds the optimal path while exploring fewer vertices than Dijkstra. On a grid, Manhattan or Euclidean distance is the natural heuristic; for a road network, straight-line distance divided by the maximum speed is. A heuristic that overestimates makes the search faster and the answer wrong, which is a trade that should be made deliberately rather than accidentally.

When edges have negative weights, use Bellman-Ford: relax every edge repeatedly for as many rounds as there are vertices, and if a further relaxation is still possible the graph contains a negative cycle reachable from the source. For all-pairs shortest paths on a small dense graph, Floyd-Warshall runs three nested loops over the vertex count and is the simplest correct implementation. Above a few hundred vertices it becomes impractical, and the right answer is usually to run Dijkstra from each vertex or to change the problem.

### Sorting and searching

Array.prototype.sort sorts in place and converts every element to a string by default, which is why [10, 9, 80].sort() produces [10, 80, 9]. A comparator must be supplied for anything that is not a string, and the comparator must return a negative number, zero or a positive number. The specification now requires a stable sort, so equal elements keep their relative order and a multi-key sort can be composed by sorting by the least significant key first. The engine uses a hybrid that switches strategies on the size and shape of the input.

The taught comparison sorts are quicksort, mergesort and heapsort. Quicksort partitions around a pivot and is the fastest in practice because of cache behaviour, but it is quadratic on adversarial input unless the pivot is chosen randomly or by median-of-three. Mergesort is stable and predictable at O(n log n) but needs auxiliary space. Heapsort is in place and worst-case optimal but has poor locality. The non-comparison sorts — counting sort, radix sort and bucket sort — can beat all three when the key space is small and bounded, which is common for integers.

Writing a correct quicksort means getting the partition right, and there are two schemes worth knowing. Lomuto's partition is four lines and does more swaps; Hoare's is subtler and does fewer. Both must guarantee termination when many elements are equal to the pivot, which is why the modern advice is a three-way partition into less than, equal and greater than. Without it, an array of identical values produces the worst case on the standard two-way scheme. This is the single most common quicksort bug.

Naive recursion recomputes the same subproblems; memoisation caches by argument so each is solved once:

```js
const fib = (n) => (n < 2 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2));

function memo(fn, cache = new Map()) {
  return function (...args) {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);
    if (!cache.has(key)) cache.set(key, fn.apply(this, args));
    return cache.get(key);
  };
}
const fastFib = memo((n) => (n < 2 ? n : fastFib(n - 1) + fastFib(n - 2)));
console.log(fib(20), fastFib(90));   // 6765 2880067194370816120
```

Tabulation turns the recursion inside out and removes the call-stack limit entirely:

```js
function fibTable(n) {
  let prev = 0, cur = 1;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) [prev, cur] = [cur, prev + cur];
  return prev;
}
console.log(fibTable(90));   // 2880067194370816120

function makeChange(coins, amount) {
  const dp = new Array(amount + 1).fill(Infinity);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let total = 1; total <= amount; total++) {
    for (const coin of coins) {
      if (coin <= total) dp[total] = Math.min(dp[total], dp[total - coin] + 1);
    }
  }
  return dp[amount] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[amount];
}
console.log(makeChange([1, 5, 10, 25], 63));   // 6
```

The 0/1 knapsack chooses a subset under a weight limit, with one row per item and one column per capacity:

```js
function knapsack(items, capacity) {
  const dp = Array.from({ length: items.length + 1 }, () => new Array(capacity + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= items.length; i++) {
    const { weight, value } = items[i - 1];
    for (let c = 0; c <= capacity; c++) {
      dp[i][c] = dp[i - 1][c];
      if (weight <= c) dp[i][c] = Math.max(dp[i][c], dp[i - 1][c - weight] + value);
    }
  }
  return dp[items.length][capacity];
}
console.log(knapsack([
  { weight: 1, value: 1 }, { weight: 3, value: 4 }, { weight: 4, value: 5 }, { weight: 5, value: 7 },
], 7));   // 9
```

Longest common subsequence and edit distance differ only in their cost model, and both are two-dimensional:

```js
function lcs(a, b) {
  const dp = Array.from({ length: a.length + 1 }, () => new Array(b.length + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= a.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= b.length; j++) {
      dp[i][j] = a[i - 1] === b[j - 1]
        ? dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
        : Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
    }
  }
  return dp[a.length][b.length];
}
console.log(lcs('AGGTAB', 'GXTXAYB'));   // 4

function editDistance(a, b) {
  const dp = Array.from({ length: a.length + 1 }, (_, i) => [i, ...new Array(b.length).fill(0)]);
  for (let j = 0; j <= b.length; j++) dp[0][j] = j;
  for (let i = 1; i <= a.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= b.length; j++) {
      dp[i][j] = a[i - 1] === b[j - 1]
        ? dp[i - 1][j - 1]
        : 1 + Math.min(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1], dp[i - 1][j - 1]);
    }
  }
  return dp[a.length][b.length];
}
console.log(editDistance('kitten', 'sitting'));   // 3
```

### Memoisation and caching

Memoisation is a cache keyed on the arguments of a pure function, and its validity depends entirely on that purity. The standard wrapper serialises the arguments into a key, checks the cache, computes on a miss and stores. JSON.stringify of the argument array is the common key derivation and it fails on functions, on undefined values that disappear from a serialised array, on values that stringify identically but differ, and on property order. A hand-written key function for the specific arguments is safer whenever the shape is known.

The cache should be a Map rather than an object, so keys can be of any type and insertion order is preserved for eviction. A plain object forces string keys, shares the prototype chain, and has surprising behaviour for keys such as __proto__. A WeakMap is the right choice when the single argument is an object and the memoised result should live exactly as long as that object, which avoids an unbounded cache entirely. For multiple object arguments, nest WeakMaps or fall back to a bounded Map.

The hardest part is bounding the cache. An unbounded memo is a memory leak with a friendly name, and it is a common one because the growth is invisible until it is fatal. Options are a maximum size with least-recently-used eviction, a time-to-live, or a WeakMap where the key lifetime governs. LRU eviction needs the access order, which a Map gives for free by deleting and reinserting on a hit. A TTL needs a timestamp per entry, which means storing an object rather than the raw value.

In dynamic programming, memoisation and tabulation are the two forms of the same idea. Top-down memoisation starts from the full problem and recurses into subproblems, caching each; bottom-up tabulation starts from the base cases and fills a table forward until the answer is reached. Top-down is easier to derive because it mirrors the recurrence, and it only computes the subproblems that are actually reachable. Bottom-up avoids recursion depth entirely and has better locality, and it is easier to optimise the space down to a few rows when each state depends only on the previous ones.

Recognising a dynamic programming problem is about structure: overlapping subproblems, where the same smaller question is asked repeatedly, and optimal substructure, where the answer to a larger question is built from optimal answers to smaller ones. The recurrence is the specification and the code should follow it literally in the top-down version. Knapsack, edit distance, longest common subsequence, coin change and shortest paths in a DAG are the canonical shapes, and each has a one-dimensional table plus two nested loops as its bottom-up form.

Space optimisation is where the bottom-up form shines. If each row of the table depends only on the previous row, two rows suffice; if each cell depends only on the previous and the current cell, one row suffices, provided the update order within the row is correct for the direction of dependency. Getting that direction wrong produces an answer that is plausible and wrong, which is worse than a crash. When optimising the space, always keep the full-table version in the test suite and compare the two on random inputs before trusting the compact one.

Dependencies should point inward, so a domain module never imports the database or the HTTP layer that calls it:

```js
// domain/order.js — pure rules, no I/O
const TAX = 0.2;
function priceOf(items) {
  const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.qty, 0);
  return { subtotal, tax: subtotal * TAX, total: subtotal * (1 + TAX) };
}
export { priceOf };

// app/api.js — adapts HTTP to the domain
import { priceOf } from '../domain/order.js';
const priceHandler = (req) => ({ status: 200, body: priceOf(req.body.items) });
export { priceHandler };
```

A composition root wires the concrete implementations once, so the rest of the app depends only on interfaces:

```js
function createApp({ repo, mailer, clock = () => new Date() }) {
  async function register(email) {
    const user = { email, createdAt: clock().toISOString() };
    await repo.save(user);
    await mailer.send(email, 'welcome');
    return user;
  }
  return { register };
}

const app = createApp({
  repo: { save: async (user) => console.log('saved', user.email) },
  mailer: { send: async (to, kind) => console.log('sent', kind, 'to', to) },
});
await app.register('a@example.com');
```

Feature folders keep everything a module owns together, and a single entry point stops callers reaching into internals:

```js
// users/index.js — the only supported import surface
import { createUserService } from './service.js';
export { createUserService };

// main.js
import { createUserService } from './users/index.js';
const users = createUserService({});
console.log(typeof users.list === 'function');
```

An error boundary is just a wrapper that attaches context without changing the shape of the value:

```js
const withContext = (fn, context) => async (...args) => {
  try {
    return await fn(...args);
  } catch (err) {
    err.context = { ...context, args: args.length };
    throw err;
  }
};
const save = withContext(async () => { throw new Error('db down'); }, { layer: 'users' });
await save().catch((err) => console.log(err.message, err.context));
```

### Configuration and environments

Read configuration once, at startup, validate it, and freeze it into a typed object. Everything downstream receives values rather than looking up environment variables, which means the failure for a missing variable happens at boot with a clear message instead of during a request as an undefined. Validation should check presence, type and range, and should apply defaults explicitly so that the set of defaults is visible in one place. A configuration module that throws on an invalid value is doing the most valuable work in the application.

Separate configuration from secrets. Configuration is not sensitive — a log level, a port, a feature flag — and belongs in a committed file with defaults. Secrets are credentials and belong in the environment or a secret manager, never in the repository and never in a build artifact. Both arrive by the same mechanism in most deployments, but the distinction still matters for what may be logged, what may be bundled for the client, and what must be rotated. A value that reaches the client bundle is public; treating anything else as secret is a bug.

Distinguish build-time from run-time values. A value substituted by the bundler is baked into the artifact and cannot change without a rebuild, while a value read at run time can change with a restart. Which you want depends on whether the value must vary per environment for the same artifact. A single artifact promoted through environments should read its configuration at run time; an artifact built per environment may inline it. Mixing the two is how a staging URL ends up in production.

Feature flags deserve a policy. A flag is a conditional with a lifetime, so every flag needs an owner and a removal date. Flags that stay forever become untested branches that nobody dares delete. Naming flags for the behaviour rather than the release makes them easier to grep and to reason about. Defaulting to the safe state when the flag service is unreachable is what keeps an outage in the flag system from becoming an outage in the application.

Environment parity reduces surprises. Development, staging and production should differ in configuration values, not in code paths. A branch that runs only in production is a branch that is never exercised, and a database with a different version in staging is a migration that will fail when it is run for the first time. Keeping the shapes identical and the values different is the cheapest way to make staging predictive. The remaining unavoidable differences should be listed and tested deliberately.

Report the effective configuration without reporting the secrets. A startup log line with the environment name, the version, the log level, the feature flag state and the endpoints being contacted makes a bug report actionable and reveals a wrong-value misconfiguration immediately. Redact by key name, print the names of the secrets that were found rather than their values, and include the source of each value when there is more than one layer. This is one of the few logs that should always be on.

A reducer keeps every transition in one place, and immutability is what makes change detection cheap:

```js
function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'add': return { ...state, items: [...state.items, action.item] };
    case 'remove': return { ...state, items: state.items.filter((i) => i.id !== action.id) };
    case 'toggle': return { ...state, done: !state.done };
    default: return state;
  }
}
let state = { items: [], done: false };
state = reducer(state, { type: 'add', item: { id: 1 } });
state = reducer(state, { type: 'toggle' });
console.log(state);   // { items: [ { id: 1 } ], done: true }
```

Immutable updates by hand get verbose, so a structural-sharing helper pays for itself:

```js
const setIn = (obj, path, value) => {
  if (!path.length) return value;
  const [key, ...rest] = path;
  return { ...obj, [key]: setIn(obj[key] ?? {}, rest, value) };
};
const original = { theme: 'dark', prefs: { fontSize: 14 } };
const updated = setIn(original, ['prefs', 'fontSize'], 16);
console.log(updated.prefs.fontSize, original.prefs.fontSize);   // 16 14
console.log(updated.prefs === original.prefs);                  // false
```

A time-to-live cache is the simplest memo that is still correct in a long-running process:

```js
function ttlCache(load, ttlMs = 30_000) {
  const store = new Map();
  return async (key) => {
    const hit = store.get(key);
    if (hit && hit.expires > Date.now()) return hit.value;
    const value = await load(key);
    store.set(key, { value, expires: Date.now() + ttlMs });
    return value;
  };
}
let calls = 0;
const slow = ttlCache(async (key) => { calls++; return key.toUpperCase(); }, 1000);
await slow('a'); await slow('a');
console.log(calls);   // 1
```

Stale-while-revalidate returns the cached value immediately and refreshes in the background, which keeps a UI responsive:

```js
function staleWhileRevalidate(load, maxAgeMs) {
  const store = new Map();
  const inflight = new Map();
  return (key) => {
    const hit = store.get(key);
    const fresh = hit && Date.now() - hit.at < maxAgeMs;
    if (!inflight.has(key)) {
      inflight.set(key, load(key).then((value) => {
        store.set(key, { value, at: Date.now() });
        inflight.delete(key);
      }));
    }
    return fresh ? Promise.resolve(hit.value) : inflight.get(key);
  };
}
const load = staleWhileRevalidate(async (k) => k.length, 50);
console.log(await load('abc'));
```

### Caching layers

A cache is a bet that the same question will be asked again before the answer changes. Every cache therefore needs an invalidation policy, and the policy is the hard part. Time-based expiry is the simplest and sacrifices freshness. Key-based invalidation is precise and requires knowing every key a write affects. Version-tagging invalidates everything at once by changing a prefix. Choosing between them is a product decision about how stale an answer may be, and it should be written down rather than inferred from the code.

Cache at the layer closest to the consumer that has enough information to invalidate. A memoised pure function is safe because its inputs determine its output. A per-request cache is safe because the request ends. A per-user cache in server memory is dangerous in a clustered deployment because the same user may hit different processes, and a shared cache is dangerous without an eviction policy because it grows forever. Naming the layer and its lifetime in the code is what turns a cache from a hazard into an optimisation.

The cache key must include everything the value depends on. Locale, currency, user role, feature flags, data version and the parameters of the query are common omissions. A missing dimension produces a correct-looking result for the wrong user, which is a security issue as well as a correctness one. Deriving the key from a single canonical function, used by both read and write paths, is the only reliable way to keep the two in agreement; a key assembled separately in two places will drift.

Cache stampedes are the failure mode to plan for. When a popular key expires, every concurrent request misses and recomputes simultaneously, and the backend sees a load spike exactly when the cache was supposed to protect it. Two standard mitigations: a short randomised jitter on the expiry so keys do not expire together, and a single-flight lock so only the first miss recomputes while the others wait or serve the stale value. The stale-while-revalidate pattern makes the trade explicit: serve slightly old data immediately and refresh in the background.

HTTP responses have their own cache vocabulary and it is worth using rather than reinventing. Cache-Control distinguishes public from private, and max-age, s-maxage and stale-while-revalidate express the policy. ETag plus If-None-Match turns a large response into a small 304. Vary names the request headers the response depends on, so a shared cache does not serve a compressed body to a client that asked for uncompressed. A correct combination of these will outperform most application-level caches because the intermediary does the work for you.

Measure the cache or remove it. Hit rate, miss rate, the cost of a miss and the memory used per entry determine whether the cache is helping. A cache with a low hit rate adds latency and memory while protecting the backend from almost nothing. Instrument the hit and miss paths, log the key on a miss when debugging, and be prepared to delete a cache that does not pay. Caches also change the profile of the system, often moving the bottleneck to the database cold path, so only a measurement tells you where you are.

Validate at the boundary and let an explicit result carry the guarantee inward, rather than throwing and hoping:

```js
function validateUser(input) {
  const errors = [];
  if (typeof input?.email !== 'string' || !input.email.includes('@')) errors.push('email');
  if (!Number.isInteger(input?.age) || input.age < 0) errors.push('age');
  return errors.length ? { ok: false, errors } : { ok: true, value: { email: input.email, age: input.age } };
}
console.log(validateUser({ email: 'a@b', age: 30 }));
console.log(validateUser({ email: 'nope', age: -1 }));
```

A Result type makes failure explicit and composable, so callers must handle it before they can read the value:

```js
const Ok = (value) => ({ ok: true, value });
const Err = (error) => ({ ok: false, error });
const map = (result, fn) => (result.ok ? Ok(fn(result.value)) : result);

const parse = (s) => { try { return Ok(JSON.parse(s)); } catch { return Err('invalid JSON'); } };
const result = map(parse('{"a":1}'), (v) => v.a + 1);
console.log(result.ok ? result.value : result.error);   // 2
```

Retries need jitter and a cap, or a recovering service gets a synchronised stampede:

```js
async function withRetry(fn, { retries = 4, base = 100, max = 5000 } = {}) {
  for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
    try { return await fn(); }
    catch (err) {
      if (attempt >= retries || err.retryable === false) throw err;
      const backoff = Math.min(max, base * 2 ** attempt);
      await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, backoff * (0.5 + Math.random() / 2)));
    }
  }
}
let n = 0;
const flaky = () => (++n < 3 ? Promise.reject(new Error('boom')) : Promise.resolve('ok'));
console.log(await withRetry(flaky));   // ok
```

A circuit breaker stops hammering a dependency that is already down, and a timeout stops you waiting forever:

```js
function breaker(fn, { threshold = 3, cooldownMs = 10_000 } = {}) {
  let failures = 0, openedAt = 0;
  return async (...args) => {
    if (failures >= threshold && Date.now() - openedAt < cooldownMs) throw new Error('circuit open');
    try { const value = await fn(...args); failures = 0; return value; }
    catch (err) { failures++; openedAt = Date.now(); throw err; }
  };
}
const guarded = breaker(async () => { throw new Error('down'); });
for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) await guarded().catch((e) => console.log(e.message));
```

Typed errors let callers branch on the kind of failure rather than on a message string:

```js
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, { code, status = 500, retryable = false } = {}) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'AppError';
    this.code = code;
    this.status = status;
    this.retryable = retryable;
  }
  toJSON() { return { code: this.code, message: this.message }; }
}
throw new AppError('rate limited', { code: 'RATE_LIMIT', status: 429, retryable: true });
```

### Error design and resilience

Model errors as values where failure is expected and as exceptions where it is not. A validation failure, a not-found and a permission denial are ordinary outcomes of a well-formed request, so returning them as typed results makes the caller handle them explicitly. An exception is for a broken invariant: a missing configuration, a corrupted response, an unreachable dependency. Getting this classification right is most of what makes error handling readable, because it separates the paths the author would think about from the ones that indicate a defect.

A custom error class carries a stable code, an HTTP status and a machine-readable detail alongside the human message. Deriving all application errors from one base lets a single handler at the boundary map them to responses without a chain of instanceof checks. The code is what clients branch on; the message is what logs and humans read; the detail is what the interface needs to highlight a field. Keeping the three separate prevents a copy edit from breaking a client integration, which is a failure mode worth designing against from the start.

Timeouts, retries and circuit breakers are the three resilience primitives, and each solves a different problem. A timeout bounds how long you wait, without which a slow dependency consumes your concurrency until you are the one who is down. A retry handles a transient failure, and must be limited to idempotent operations with exponential backoff and jitter. A circuit breaker stops calling a dependency that is failing, so that recovery is possible and so that the failure does not consume your resources on every request. All three are about protecting yourself, not about making the dependency healthy.

Bulkheads limit the blast radius by separating resources. A pool of connections per dependency, a separate queue for background work, and a concurrency limit per route mean that one saturated path cannot starve the others. Without this, a single slow endpoint can occupy every available slot and take the whole service down, a failure mode often called a cascading failure or a thread pool exhaustion. The fix is not more capacity but a boundary, and a boundary that is too generous is the same as no boundary.

Degrade rather than fail. When a recommendation service is down, show the most popular items instead of an error. When a personalisation cache is unavailable, fall back to the default ordering. When a non-essential field cannot be fetched, omit it and let the screen render. Each of these requires deciding in advance which parts of the response are essential, which is a product question with an engineering answer. A system that can serve a reduced answer is available at a level the customer can use; one that requires every dependency is only as available as its weakest one.

Finally, make failures observable at the moment they happen and reproducible afterwards. Log the request identifier, the operation, the dependency, the duration and the outcome of every failure. Emit a metric per dependency and per outcome so a rising error rate is visible before it becomes an incident. Include the version of the code and of the configuration in the log so a regression can be tied to a deploy. The cost of this instrumentation is small and the alternative is reconstructing an incident from user reports, which takes far longer and is often wrong.

Never build SQL, HTML or shell strings by concatenation; parameterise, escape, or pass an argument array:

```js
